rl":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/codeless/commit/1b541672e6abfbd85fd49fb5e7d42fea3c626861","node_id":"C_kwDOSa1B6doAKDFiNTQxNjcyZTZhYmZiZDg1ZmQ0OWZiNWU3ZDQyZmVhM2M2MjY4NjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/codeless/commit/64055dcba37d5a920a064c0e7f761639729e07be","sha":"64055dcba37d5a920a064c0e7f761639729e07be","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/64055dcba37d5a920a064c0e7f761639729e07be"}],"sha":"1b541672e6abfbd85fd49fb5e7d42fea3c626861","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/1b541672e6abfbd85fd49fb5e7d42fea3c626861"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/64055dcba37d5a920a064c0e7f761639729e07be/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-05-19T01:31:15Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-05-19T01:31:15Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"adapter-registry (stage 1): SQLite-backed chat/runner enablement, six new RPCs, graceful restart (#36)\n\n* stage 1: resolve adapter-registry open questions\n\nRecord decisions and one-line reasoning in SCOPE.md §\"Open questions\"\n→ Settled for the four open questions called out in WORKSPACE-ATTACH.md\n§\"TODO — adapter registry\": composite-vs-single PK, --respawn-on-exit\ndefault, validate-cache lifetime, and kill-vs-resumable partition rule.\n\n* stage 0: resolve the open questions recorded in SCOPE.md §\"Open questions\" (composite-vs-single PK, --respawn-on-exit default, validate-cache lifetime, kill-vs-resumable partition rule); record each decision and one-line reasoning in SCOPE.md before any code lands (S)\n\n* stage 1: the decisions before implementation begins\n\n* stage 3: SQLite adapter_registry tables + boot-time CLI flag upsert\n\nAdd `chat_adapters((kind, instance_id) PK, enabled, configured_at)`\nand `runner_config(runner_id PK, enabled)` migration plus the\nruntime helpers that wrap upsert + read. `codeless serve` now\nupserts a row for each `--enable-*` flag at boot, then reads the\ntables back to compute the effective enabled set — the same shape\n`--fs-root` already uses against `attached_workspaces`. The flag\nkeeps working as a bootstrap convenience, but the table is the\nsource of truth once the future Settings page writes to it.\n\nStage 1 of DOCS/WORKSPACE-ATTACH.md \"TODO — adapter registry\".\nHot-reload and the matching RPC surface are explicit follow-up\nstages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 2: add the SQLite tables — chat_adapters((kind, instance_id) PK, enabled, configured_at), runner_config(runner_id PK, enabled) — plus the boot-time read that replaces the CLI flag wiring in codeless-cli/src/serve.rs; --enable-* flags become idempotent upserts (same shape as --fs-root → attached_workspaces); migration test in tests/migrations.rs (M)\n\n* stage 4: extend the SecretStore behind a SecretBackend trait — keep the XDG TOML backend as the default, add the keyring-crate backend (Secret Service / Keychain / Credential Manager) behind a config flag, and write the write-then-fsync-then-restart ordering test that crashes the process between secrets-write and the restart signal (L)\n\n`SecretStore` becomes a thin facade over a `SecretBackend` trait\nwith `load`/`save`/`location`. The TOML backend at the XDG path is\nthe default and reachable through `SecretStore::open(path)` unchanged,\nso every existing call site (CLI subcommand, slack/telegram config,\nauth bearer load, tauri boot) compiles without churn. A second\nbackend, `KeyringSecretBackend`, sits behind a non-default `keyring`\nCargo feature and talks to Secret Service / Keychain / Credential\nManager through the `keyring` v3 crate; it shadows the set of stored\nkeys in a `__codeless_index__` entry because the crate exposes no\nenumerate primitive. The keyring backend is chosen at construction\ntime (`SecretStore::open_keyring(service)`) — that is the \"config\nflag\" the doc describes; routing the CLI / settings page through it\nis follow-up work.\n\nThe exit test `write_then_fsync_survives_crash_before_restart_signal`\nproves the ordering the adapter-registry restart RPC relies on: a\nchild process spawned with `--exact crash_helper_child` writes a\nsecret, calls `save` (which fsyncs through the TOML backend), then\n`std::process::abort()`s *before* any restart signal could fire. The\nparent reopens the file and asserts the new value survived. If the\nTOML backend ever drops its fsync this test catches it; today''s\nload-bearing comment is no longer the only proof.\n\n* stage 3: extend the SecretStore behind a SecretBackend trait — keep the XDG TOML backend as the default, add the keyring-crate backend (Secret Service / Keychain / Credential Manager) behind a config flag, and write the write-then-fsync-then-restart ordering test that crashes the process between secrets-write and the restart signal (L)\n\n* stage 5: add the wire types in codeless-types and codeless-rpc — ChatAdapterKind, ChatAdapterRow, RunnerRow, ListChatAdaptersResult, SetChatAdapterEnabledArgs, ValidateChatAdapterSecretsArgs/Result, ListRunnersResult, SetRunnerEnabledArgs, RestartServerArgs/Result, MissingSecrets / ValidationFailed / RestartUnsupervised / RestartHasRunningJobs error variants — specta derives, iOS/Android-safe (S)\n\n* stage 4: add the wire types in codeless-types and codeless-rpc — ChatAdapterKind, ChatAdapterRow, RunnerRow, ListChatAdaptersResult, SetChatAdapterEnabledArgs, ValidateChatAdapterSecretsArgs/Result, ListRunnersResult, SetRunnerEnabledArgs, RestartServerArgs/Result, MissingSecrets / ValidationFailed / RestartUnsupervised / RestartHasRunningJobs error variants — specta derives, iOS/Android-safe (S)\n\n* stage 6: implement the four chat-adapter RPCs (list/set_enabled/validate/with the validate-before-enable coupling) and the two runner RPCs (list/set_enabled) end-to-end in codeless-rpc + codeless-runtime; cargo test round-trips list → validate → set_enabled → restart-arms; per-(kind, instance_id) rate limit (5/s); 5s hard timeout on validate calls\n\nFive new methods on `RpcServer` and `InProcessRpc`:\n\n- `list_chat_adapters` / `list_runners` snapshot the two adapter-\n  registry tables in deterministic `(kind, instance_id)` /\n  `runner_id` order so the future Settings page renders the same\n  sequence regardless of insertion order.\n- `set_chat_adapter_enabled(true)` is gated by an in-memory\n  validated set keyed on `(kind, instance_id)`. The set lives for\n  the lifetime of the process (OQ#3 decision: cache = process\n  lifetime, cleared on restart, deliberate re-validate UX); a\n  refused enable returns `AdapterError::MissingSecrets { keys }`\n  carrying the canonical `secrets.toml` key names for the kind.\n  Disable is always allowed so a broken adapter can be turned off\n  without a live validate; toggling off forgets the cache entry so\n  re-enable re-proves the credentials.\n- `validate_chat_adapter_secrets` wraps a pluggable\n  `ValidationProbe` in a `tokio::time::timeout` (5s) and a sliding-\n  window 5/s per-`(kind, instance_id)` rate-limit bucket. Timeouts\n  surface as `ChatAdapterSecretProblem::Timeout` (an observation\n  the UI renders), rate-limit breaches as `RpcError::Conflict`\n  (the call never reached the upstream). A successful result is\n  what arms the validated cache; a failure clears it.\n- `set_runner_enabled` is the simpler upsert path — runners are\n  local binaries, not credentialed services, so no validate step.\n\nWire-up:\n\n- `codeless-rpc::RpcServer` grows the five methods plus re-exports\n  of `ListChatAdaptersResult`, `ListRunnersResult`,\n  `SetChatAdapterEnabledArgs`, `SetRunnerEnabledArgs`,\n  `ValidateChatAdapterSecretsArgs`, `ValidateChatAdapterSecretsResult`.\n- `codeless-runtime::rpc::adapters` owns the in-process impl and\n  the `ValidationProbe` trait; `InProcessRpc` gains `validation` +\n  `validation_probe` fields and a `with_validation_probe` builder.\n  `StaticValidationProbe` is a test seam — tests inject a static\n  result so the round-trip exercise does not touch the network.\n- `codeless-client::HttpRpcClient` and `codeless-server::routes`\n  pick up the five `/rpc/<method>` routes behind the bearer gate.\n- `codeless-cli::serve` brings its prompt-helper import behind\n  `#[cfg(test)]` so the stage-3 inner-test reference compiles\n  without polluting the lib build.\n\nTests (`crates/codeless-runtime/tests/adapter_registry_rpc.rs`):\n\n1. `list → validate → set_enabled` round-trip arms the row;\n   the subsequent `list_chat_adapters` shows it enabled. A\n   disable-then-re-enable refuses with `MissingSecrets`, proving\n   the cache invalidates on toggle-off.\n2. `set_enabled(true)` without a prior validate returns\n   `MissingSecrets { keys: [...] }` populated from the kind''s\n   canonical secret-key names; no row is written.\n3. Failed validate (probe returns `Unauthorized`) does NOT unlock\n   enable.\n4. Per-bucket rate limit: 5 calls fit, the 6th in the same window\n   refuses with `Conflict`; a different bucket is unaffected.\n5. Timeout fires deterministically at 5s under a paused tokio\n   clock (the probe sleeps 60s).\n6. Runner round-trip: list empty → set true → list reflects →\n   set false → list reflects; empty `runner_id` is rejected with\n   `InvalidArgument`.\n\nR1 (`std::process` / `tokio::process` outside\n`codeless-adapters-host`) is preserved — `codeless-runtime/src/rpc/\nadapters.rs` reaches only `parking_lot::Mutex`, `tokio::time`, and\nthe existing SQLite helpers.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 5: implement the four chat-adapter RPCs (list/set_enabled/validate/with the validate-before-enable coupling) and the two runner RPCs (list/set_enabled) end-to-end in codeless-rpc + codeless-runtime; cargo test round-trips list → validate → set_enabled → restart-arms; per-(kind, instance_id) rate limit (5/s); 5s hard timeout on validate calls (L)\n\n* stage 7: implement restart_server — three contexts (supervised CLI exits 75 EX_TEMPFAIL, Tauri desktop sidecar kill-and-respawn, bare CLI returns RestartUnsupervised); add codeless serve --respawn-on-exit (self-watcher parent that re-execs the child on exit-75) so the bare case Just Works; partition running jobs into resumable vs killed and return RestartHasRunningJobs unless force=true; integration test asserts the partition (M)\n\n* stage 6: implement restart_server — three contexts (supervised CLI exits 75 EX_TEMPFAIL, Tauri desktop sidecar kill-and-respawn, bare CLI returns RestartUnsupervised); add codeless serve --respawn-on-exit (self-watcher parent that re-execs the child on exit-75) so the bare case Just Works; partition running jobs into resumable vs killed and return RestartHasRunningJobs unless force=true; integration test asserts the partition (M)\n\n* stage 8: lift Slack/Telegram spawn into ChatAdapterRegistry, runner enable bits into RunnerConfig\n\nReplace the inline `if effective.slack_enabled { ... }` and\n`if effective.telegram_enabled { ... }` blocks in\n`codeless-cli/src/serve.rs` with a single `ChatAdapterRegistry::spawn`\ncall, and replace the four `enable_*: bool` fields on\n`DefaultRunnerFactory` with a `config: RunnerConfig` struct sourced\nfrom `RunnerConfig::from_effective(&EffectiveAdapterRegistry)`.\n\nThe `chat_adapters` and `runner_config` tables are now the single\nsource of truth for both surfaces; `--enable-*` CLI flags remain\nidempotent upsert shims (same pattern as `--fs-root` →\n`attached_workspaces` from milestone 2). No behavioural change beyond\nthe source of truth — the diagnostic warnings, the `mock`-gating, and\nthe runner dispatch all match the prior implementation.\n\nChatAdapterRegistry lives in `codeless-cli` rather than\n`codeless-runtime` because the runtime crate has no dependency on\n`codeless-slack` / `codeless-telegram` and we keep it that way (R1).\nRunnerConfig lives in `codeless-runtime` alongside the factory it\nconfigures so future tests can construct it without pulling the CLI.\n\n* stage 7: lift the Slack and Telegram adapter spawn calls out of codeless-cli/src/serve.rs into a boot-time ChatAdapterRegistry driven by the chat_adapters table; lift the DefaultRunnerFactory enable_* fields out of serve.rs into a RunnerConfig driven by the runner_config table; the --enable-* flags continue to work as upsert shims; no behavioural change beyond the source of truth (M)\n\n* stage 9: server-side milestones complete; flag Gmail adapter and stage 2 (hot-reload) as separate follow-up jobs\n\nREVIEW gate verdict: PASS. R1/R2/R4/R5 invariants hold across the\nstage 3-8 diff. Process spawning stays in codeless-adapters-host\n(the lone new Command::new() lives in respawn::supervise); every\nnew RPC rides the bearer-gated HTTP+SSE transport; the new\nchat_adapters / runner_config tables are the source of truth with\nthe in-memory validate cache as a session-lifetime gate; and\nwire.ts.snap is additive-only (no removals from any pre-existing\nTypeScript binding).\n\nDOCS/WORKSPACE-ATTACH.md updated: stage 1 checkboxes 1-5 ticked\n(step 6 Settings UI remains the follow-up job''s to tick); the\nstage-2 paragraph now names this job as the seam-provider for a\nfuture Arc<ArcSwap<RunnerConfig>> swap; the Gmail paragraph\nreworded from \"separate milestone\" to \"separate follow-up job\"\nto match the actual planning shape. Handover rewritten for the\nnext session.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 8: server-side milestones complete; flag Gmail adapter and stage 2 (hot-reload) as separate follow-up jobs\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"18a231ff7a9a65968b7210a31ce6568d6708cd66","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/git/trees/18a231ff7a9a65968b7210a31ce6568d6708cd66"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/git/commits/64055dcba37d5a920a064c0e7f761639729e07be","verification":{"payload":"tree 18a231ff7a9a65968b7210a31ce6568d6708cd66\nparent a8cb35d4c728fa3aabe61e3179b8c4ac05c86354\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1779154275 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1779154275 +0700\n\nadapter-registry (stage 1): SQLite-backed chat/runner enablement, six new RPCs, graceful restart (#36)\n\n* stage 1: resolve adapter-registry open questions\n\nRecord decisions and one-line reasoning in SCOPE.md §\"Open questions\"\n→ Settled for the four open questions called out in WORKSPACE-ATTACH.md\n§\"TODO — adapter registry\": composite-vs-single PK, --respawn-on-exit\ndefault, validate-cache lifetime, and kill-vs-resumable partition rule.\n\n* stage 0: resolve the open questions recorded in SCOPE.md §\"Open questions\" (composite-vs-single PK, --respawn-on-exit default, validate-cache lifetime, kill-vs-resumable partition rule); record each decision and one-line reasoning in SCOPE.md before any code lands (S)\n\n* stage 1: the decisions before implementation begins\n\n* stage 3: SQLite adapter_registry tables + boot-time CLI flag upsert\n\nAdd `chat_adapters((kind, instance_id) PK, enabled, configured_at)`\nand `runner_config(runner_id PK, enabled)` migration plus the\nruntime helpers that wrap upsert + read. `codeless serve` now\nupserts a row for each `--enable-*` flag at boot, then reads the\ntables back to compute the effective enabled set — the same shape\n`--fs-root` already uses against `attached_workspaces`. The flag\nkeeps working as a bootstrap convenience, but the table is the\nsource of truth once the future Settings page writes to it.\n\nStage 1 of DOCS/WORKSPACE-ATTACH.md \"TODO — adapter registry\".\nHot-reload and the matching RPC surface are explicit follow-up\nstages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 2: add the SQLite tables — chat_adapters((kind, instance_id) PK, enabled, configured_at), runner_config(runner_id PK, enabled) — plus the boot-time read that replaces the CLI flag wiring in codeless-cli/src/serve.rs; --enable-* flags become idempotent upserts (same shape as --fs-root → attached_workspaces); migration test in tests/migrations.rs (M)\n\n* stage 4: extend the SecretStore behind a SecretBackend trait — keep the XDG TOML backend as the default, add the keyring-crate backend (Secret Service / Keychain / Credential Manager) behind a config flag, and write the write-then-fsync-then-restart ordering test that crashes the process between secrets-write and the restart signal (L)\n\n`SecretStore` becomes a thin facade over a `SecretBackend` trait\nwith `load`/`save`/`location`. The TOML backend at the XDG path is\nthe default and reachable through `SecretStore::open(path)` unchanged,\nso every existing call site (CLI subcommand, slack/telegram config,\nauth bearer load, tauri boot) compiles without churn. A second\nbackend, `KeyringSecretBackend`, sits behind a non-default `keyring`\nCargo feature and talks to Secret Service / Keychain / Credential\nManager through the `keyring` v3 crate; it shadows the set of stored\nkeys in a `__codeless_index__` entry because the crate exposes no\nenumerate primitive. The keyring backend is chosen at construction\ntime (`SecretStore::open_keyring(service)`) — that is the \"config\nflag\" the doc describes; routing the CLI / settings page through it\nis follow-up work.\n\nThe exit test `write_then_fsync_survives_crash_before_restart_signal`\nproves the ordering the adapter-registry restart RPC relies on: a\nchild process spawned with `--exact crash_helper_child` writes a\nsecret, calls `save` (which fsyncs through the TOML backend), then\n`std::process::abort()`s *before* any restart signal could fire. The\nparent reopens the file and asserts the new value survived. If the\nTOML backend ever drops its fsync this test catches it; today''s\nload-bearing comment is no longer the only proof.\n\n* stage 3: extend the SecretStore behind a SecretBackend trait — keep the XDG TOML backend as the default, add the keyring-crate backend (Secret Service / Keychain / Credential Manager) behind a config flag, and write the write-then-fsync-then-restart ordering test that crashes the process between secrets-write and the restart signal (L)\n\n* stage 5: add the wire types in codeless-types and codeless-rpc — ChatAdapterKind, ChatAdapterRow, RunnerRow, ListChatAdaptersResult, SetChatAdapterEnabledArgs, ValidateChatAdapterSecretsArgs/Result, ListRunnersResult, SetRunnerEnabledArgs, RestartServerArgs/Result, MissingSecrets / ValidationFailed / RestartUnsupervised / RestartHasRunningJobs error variants — specta derives, iOS/Android-safe (S)\n\n* stage 4: add the wire types in codeless-types and codeless-rpc — ChatAdapterKind, ChatAdapterRow, RunnerRow, ListChatAdaptersResult, SetChatAdapterEnabledArgs, ValidateChatAdapterSecretsArgs/Result, ListRunnersResult, SetRunnerEnabledArgs, RestartServerArgs/Result, MissingSecrets / ValidationFailed / RestartUnsupervised / RestartHasRunningJobs error variants — specta derives, iOS/Android-safe (S)\n\n* stage 6: implement the four chat-adapter RPCs (list/set_enabled/validate/with the validate-before-enable coupling) and the two runner RPCs (list/set_enabled) end-to-end in codeless-rpc + codeless-runtime; cargo test round-trips list → validate → set_enabled → restart-arms; per-(kind, instance_id) rate limit (5/s); 5s hard timeout on validate calls\n\nFive new methods on `RpcServer` and `InProcessRpc`:\n\n- `list_chat_adapters` / `list_runners` snapshot the two adapter-\n  registry tables in deterministic `(kind, instance_id)` /\n  `runner_id` order so the future Settings page renders the same\n  sequence regardless of insertion order.\n- `set_chat_adapter_enabled(true)` is gated by an in-memory\n  validated set keyed on `(kind, instance_id)`. The set lives for\n  the lifetime of the process (OQ#3 decision: cache = process\n  lifetime, cleared on restart, deliberate re-validate UX); a\n  refused enable returns `AdapterError::MissingSecrets { keys }`\n  carrying the canonical `secrets.toml` key names for the kind.\n  Disable is always allowed so a broken adapter can be turned off\n  without a live validate; toggling off forgets the cache entry so\n  re-enable re-proves the credentials.\n- `validate_chat_adapter_secrets` wraps a pluggable\n  `ValidationProbe` in a `tokio::time::timeout` (5s) and a sliding-\n  window 5/s per-`(kind, instance_id)` rate-limit bucket. Timeouts\n  surface as `ChatAdapterSecretProblem::Timeout` (an observation\n  the UI renders), rate-limit breaches as `RpcError::Conflict`\n  (the call never reached the upstream). A successful result is\n  what arms the validated cache; a failure clears it.\n- `set_runner_enabled` is the simpler upsert path — runners are\n  local binaries, not credentialed services, so no validate step.\n\nWire-up:\n\n- `codeless-rpc::RpcServer` grows the five methods plus re-exports\n  of `ListChatAdaptersResult`, `ListRunnersResult`,\n  `SetChatAdapterEnabledArgs`, `SetRunnerEnabledArgs`,\n  `ValidateChatAdapterSecretsArgs`, `ValidateChatAdapterSecretsResult`.\n- `codeless-runtime::rpc::adapters` owns the in-process impl and\n  the `ValidationProbe` trait; `InProcessRpc` gains `validation` +\n  `validation_probe` fields and a `with_validation_probe` builder.\n  `StaticValidationProbe` is a test seam — tests inject a static\n  result so the round-trip exercise does not touch the network.\n- `codeless-client::HttpRpcClient` and `codeless-server::routes`\n  pick up the five `/rpc/<method>` routes behind the bearer gate.\n- `codeless-cli::serve` brings its prompt-helper import behind\n  `#[cfg(test)]` so the stage-3 inner-test reference compiles\n  without polluting the lib build.\n\nTests (`crates/codeless-runtime/tests/adapter_registry_rpc.rs`):\n\n1. `list → validate → set_enabled` round-trip arms the row;\n   the subsequent `list_chat_adapters` shows it enabled. A\n   disable-then-re-enable refuses with `MissingSecrets`, proving\n   the cache invalidates on toggle-off.\n2. `set_enabled(true)` without a prior validate returns\n   `MissingSecrets { keys: [...] }` populated from the kind''s\n   canonical secret-key names; no row is written.\n3. Failed validate (probe returns `Unauthorized`) does NOT unlock\n   enable.\n4. Per-bucket rate limit: 5 calls fit, the 6th in the same window\n   refuses with `Conflict`; a different bucket is unaffected.\n5. Timeout fires deterministically at 5s under a paused tokio\n   clock (the probe sleeps 60s).\n6. Runner round-trip: list empty → set true → list reflects →\n   set false → list reflects; empty `runner_id` is rejected with\n   `InvalidArgument`.\n\nR1 (`std::process` / `tokio::process` outside\n`codeless-adapters-host`) is preserved — `codeless-runtime/src/rpc/\nadapters.rs` reaches only `parking_lot::Mutex`, `tokio::time`, and\nthe existing SQLite helpers.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 5: implement the four chat-adapter RPCs (list/set_enabled/validate/with the validate-before-enable coupling) and the two runner RPCs (list/set_enabled) end-to-end in codeless-rpc + codeless-runtime; cargo test round-trips list → validate → set_enabled → restart-arms; per-(kind, instance_id) rate limit (5/s); 5s hard timeout on validate calls (L)\n\n* stage 7: implement restart_server — three contexts (supervised CLI exits 75 EX_TEMPFAIL, Tauri desktop sidecar kill-and-respawn, bare CLI returns RestartUnsupervised); add codeless serve --respawn-on-exit (self-watcher parent that re-execs the child on exit-75) so the bare case Just Works; partition running jobs into resumable vs killed and return RestartHasRunningJobs unless force=true; integration test asserts the partition (M)\n\n* stage 6: implement restart_server — three contexts (supervised CLI exits 75 EX_TEMPFAIL, Tauri desktop sidecar kill-and-respawn, bare CLI returns RestartUnsupervised); add codeless serve --respawn-on-exit (self-watcher parent that re-execs the child on exit-75) so the bare case Just Works; partition running jobs into resumable vs killed and return RestartHasRunningJobs unless force=true; integration test asserts the partition (M)\n\n* stage 8: lift Slack/Telegram spawn into ChatAdapterRegistry, runner enable bits into RunnerConfig\n\nReplace the inline `if effective.slack_enabled { ... }` and\n`if effective.telegram_enabled { ... }` blocks in\n`codeless-cli/src/serve.rs` with a single `ChatAdapterRegistry::spawn`\ncall, and replace the four `enable_*: bool` fields on\n`DefaultRunnerFactory` with a `config: RunnerConfig` struct sourced\nfrom `RunnerConfig::from_effective(&EffectiveAdapterRegistry)`.\n\nThe `chat_adapters` and `runner_config` tables are now the single\nsource of truth for both surfaces; `--enable-*` CLI flags remain\nidempotent upsert shims (same pattern as `--fs-root` →\n`attached_workspaces` from milestone 2). No behavioural change beyond\nthe source of truth — the diagnostic warnings, the `mock`-gating, and\nthe runner dispatch all match the prior implementation.\n\nChatAdapterRegistry lives in `codeless-cli` rather than\n`codeless-runtime` because the runtime crate has no dependency on\n`codeless-slack` / `codeless-telegram` and we keep it that way (R1).\nRunnerConfig lives in `codeless-runtime` alongside the factory it\nconfigures so future tests can construct it without pulling the CLI.\n\n* stage 7: lift the Slack and Telegram adapter spawn calls out of codeless-cli/src/serve.rs into a boot-time ChatAdapterRegistry driven by the chat_adapters table; lift the DefaultRunnerFactory enable_* fields out of serve.rs into a RunnerConfig driven by the runner_config table; the --enable-* flags continue to work as upsert shims; no behavioural change beyond the source of truth (M)\n\n* stage 9: server-side milestones complete; flag Gmail adapter and stage 2 (hot-reload) as separate follow-up jobs\n\nREVIEW gate verdict: PASS. R1/R2/R4/R5 invariants hold across the\nstage 3-8 diff. Process spawning stays in codeless-adapters-host\n(the lone new Command::new() lives in respawn::supervise); every\nnew RPC rides the bearer-gated HTTP+SSE transport; the new\nchat_adapters / runner_config tables are the source of truth with\nthe in-memory validate cache as a session-lifetime gate; and\nwire.ts.snap is additive-only (no removals from any pre-existing\nTypeScript binding).\n\nDOCS/WORKSPACE-ATTACH.md updated: stage 1 checkboxes 1-5 ticked\n(step 6 Settings UI remains the follow-up job''s to tick); the\nstage-2 paragraph now names this job as the seam-provider for a\nfuture Arc<ArcSwap<RunnerConfig>> swap; the Gmail paragraph\nreworded from \"separate milestone\" to \"separate follow-up job\"\nto match the actual planning shape. Handover rewritten for the\nnext session.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 8: server-side milestones complete; flag Gmail adapter and stage 2 (hot-reload) as separate follow-up jobs\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqC71jCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAe80QADMqr7Xc30odr6kHf0DMEnZB\nlb1ZNFBkjgTL2UCIgpntIQeh5FV7QELqx29erRyYPE/gTGD75kB+4yKzQ4lYki32\njt72legf6tnqIoT6vl7eRDRiBcRQnIc2IC+5Z6R08pOW8p79Vu7EuwYp3OtLkPRy\nw8QrAk/BjW9U1bFo1gNiFGj6U07xICqMjSBj1jNtSz28aAMCK6kOKdS+UCOUpI3L\nIY0hbEM/koHfic/LWjAga4I4tIAy4JSmof/4VBSlyN0sV8qL/2wrU5G6dW3h02w6\n1Qlg/i67uLhQ9dLm7UIlLMpTUwEq6oNbPgjL4aX/I9PKhkOz0bwDDGBPrsgxkk9W\nka3xYeMZ+9Tf9Szpgo5FP5uLXXG+IWd6XBQwU6f3WxSfScNQRdst9NycWIVZ4/J3\n96hmiyfx67X8xDVbwUA25i9rRuSWRc/uHOkp5hJ4hhatRGa2kzVvLaqGaesRLky/\np6jLM7F1VPDvDpVaRZUAQeDojJrfSCh6P7B/imiVBnMaPGhRXaGT2TTVyfc2D4n/\nBWT7JwQCDBmY4n4J3Q2ue3v20sHtIgpU11Ulf8slw4AO6Lfc43KTvDguaaz5k+Da\nQWMuLXYNXO2+MZeYoalrY/SXiF4F5EO+Ijkx9zmO7EkrVET7d15OVkIGalGnw/bp\nDE/C3v+2MxsjPxYYNpLv\n=MbCo\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-05-19T01:31:16Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/codeless/commit/64055dcba37d5a920a064c0e7f761639729e07be","node_id":"C_kwDOSa1B6doAKDY0MDU1ZGNiYTM3ZDVhOTIwYTA2NGMwZTdmNzYxNjM5NzI5ZTA3YmU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/codeless/commit/a8cb35d4c728fa3aabe61e3179b8c4ac05c86354","sha":"a8cb35d4c728fa3aabe61e3179b8c4ac05c86354","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/a8cb35d4c728fa3aabe61e3179b8c4ac05c86354"}],"sha":"64055dcba37d5a920a064c0e7f761639729e07be","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/64055dcba37d5a920a064c0e7f761639729e07be"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/a8cb35d4c728fa3aabe61e3179b8c4ac05c86354/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-05-19T01:30:46Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-05-19T01:30:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"auto-bypass-hardening: infra-error halt, WAL pool, precheck tokenizer, threaded bypass comment, ~ glyph (#35)\n\n* stage 1: resolve open questions\n\nFlip the four §\"Open questions\" in\n.codeless/jobs/auto-bypass-hardening/SCOPE.md from open to resolved,\neach with a chosen answer and a *why*. No code lands; this is the\nsingle source of truth the rest of the job cites.\n\n- Q1 (SQLite error codes -> InfrastructureError): five primary codes\n  — 8 READONLY, 10 IOERR, 11 CORRUPT, 13 FULL, 14 CANTOPEN.\n  Explicit exclusion list and the deliberate carve-out for\n  26 NOTADB documented inline.\n- Q2 (WAL pragma timing): per-acquire via after_connect; :memory:\n  skips journal_mode only; busy_timeout=5000 + synchronous=NORMAL\n  apply universally.\n- Q3 (path-shaped tokenizer rule): diff-derived repo-prefix set OR\n  worktree-resolves; self-updating; kills all four motivating-job\n  false positives without touching the diff-presence check.\n- Q4 (bypass-comment thread-through): triple-backtick fence with\n  no language tag, \"Previous-stage failure: <wire-name>\" plus\n  \"Detail: <truncated to 400 Unicode scalars with U+2026 marker>\";\n  None failure_class omits the whole block.\n\nAlso add DOCS/sessions/2026-05-19-auto-bypass-hardening.md as the\nper-stage running log and the stage-2 handover (the new\nFailureClass variant lands in codeless-types::stage and\ncodeless-runtime::store::codec, nothing else).\n\n* stage 0: stage 1: resolve open questions (the four-item set in SCOPE.md - exact SQLite error-code set that maps to InfrastructureError, WAL pragma timing, tokenizer rule for path-shaped tokens, exact wording of the bypass-comment failure_detail thread-through) and inline the resolutions in SCOPE.md; no implementation\n\n* stage 2: codeless-types - add FailureClass::InfrastructureError\n\nAdds the wire variant `infrastructure-error` between `runner-error` and\n`review-patch-invalid`. The variant exists so the auto-bypass policy\n(stage 4) can short-circuit to Halt instead of retrying SQLite\ndisk-full / IOERR / CANTOPEN / CORRUPT / READONLY - a fresh subprocess\non the same host with the same disk would just hit the same wall and\nbury the original cause.\n\n- Variant doc comment explains *why* (operator contract: infra\n  failures halt) per CLAUDE.md R2, not what it stores.\n- failure_class_label / parse_failure_class round-trip in\n  codeless-runtime/src/store/codec.rs.\n- Specta TS snapshots regenerated: codeless-types/tests/wire.ts.snap,\n  codeless-rpc/tests/wire-rpc.ts.snap, and the UI''s\n  ui/codeless-ui/src/lib/rpc/generated/wire.ts (produced by\n  `cargo run -p codeless-rpc --example wire_ts`).\n\nStage 3 (sqlx error -> FailureClass mapper) and stage 4\n(classify_stage_failure halt branch) are deliberately untouched - the\nvariant landing alone is the smallest reverting unit.\n\n* stage 1: stage 2: codeless-types - add FailureClass::InfrastructureError variant with serde wire name infrastructure-error; failure_class_label / parse_failure_class round-trip in codeless-runtime/src/store/codec.rs; specta wire snapshot regenerated; cargo test -p codeless-types green\n\n* stage 3: sqlx error -> FailureClass mapper at RunnerError emit sites\n\nAdd classify_runner_failure_reason and wire it at the three\ntemplate_runner.rs RunnerError emit sites (lines ~1051, 1494, 1579)\nso SQLITE_FULL / IOERR / CORRUPT / CANTOPEN / READONLY classify as\nInfrastructureError per the SCOPE Q1 decision. Primary-code mask\n(extended & 0xff) collapses SQLITE_IOERR_* / SQLITE_CORRUPT_VTAB /\nSQLITE_CANTOPEN_DIRTYWAL onto the right primary. Unit tests pin the\ninfra set, the exclusion set (1/5/6/19/20/21/25/26), extended-code\nmask behavior, non-sqlx reasons, and a sqlx::Error::Database round-\ntrip through Display.\n\n* stage 2: stage 3: codeless-runtime - sqlx error -> FailureClass mapper at every RunnerError emit site (template_runner.rs lines 1051, 1494, 1579 + any others surfaced by grep) so SQLITE_FULL / CANTOPEN / IOERR / CORRUPT classify as InfrastructureError; unit test pins each error code''s classification\n\n* stage 4: classify_stage_failure halts infrastructure-class failures regardless of auto_bypass_policy\n\n`classify_stage_failure` now short-circuits to `FailureAction::Halt`\nwhen the just-classified `FailureClass::InfrastructureError` arrives,\nmirroring the existing `stop_reason.is_some()` short-circuit so a\n`SQLITE_FULL` / `SQLITE_IOERR` / `SQLITE_CORRUPT` / `SQLITE_CANTOPEN`\n/ `SQLITE_READONLY` failure halts the job even under `Relentless`.\nRetrying the same SQL on the same disk would fail the same way; the\noperator must fix the host before the job can advance.\n\nThe halt branch stamps the new `StopReason::Infrastructure` variant\non the job row via `record_infrastructure_halt` (shape mirrors the\nexisting `record_thrash_halt` helper). The UI''s halt-label reader\nnow sees a structural reason instead of falling back to the\ndriver''s generic `RunnerCrash` default.\n\n- `codeless-types::StopReason` grows the `Infrastructure` variant\n  with a doc comment explaining the operator-visible contract.\n- `store/codec.rs` carries the variant on the kebab-case wire name\n  `infrastructure`.\n- `template_runner.rs::classify_stage_failure` widens its signature\n  to take the `Option<FailureClass>` the call site just put on the\n  `StageCompleted{Failed}` envelope; the classifier and the wire\n  event cannot disagree about what tripped.\n- Six call sites updated to pass their statically-known or\n  sqlx-mapped `FailureClass`.\n- Two new unit tests pin the contract: (a) infra-error halts under\n  `Relentless` and stamps `stop_reason`; (b) plain `RunnerError`\n  under the same policy still auto-bypasses and leaves\n  `stop_reason` `None`.\n- Specta wire snapshots (`wire.ts.snap`, `wire-rpc.ts.snap`)\n  regenerated under `SPECTA_UPDATE=1`; bot-core\n  `stop_reason_word` (chat + notify) carries the new variant.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 3: stage 4: classify_stage_failure in template_runner.rs short-circuits to FailureAction::Halt for FailureClass::InfrastructureError regardless of auto_bypass_policy (mirroring the existing stop_reason.is_some() short-circuit); halt writes a structured stop_reason on the job row so the UI labels it Infrastructure failure rather than a generic crash; unit test covers (a) infra-error halts even under Relentless policy and (b) RunnerError without infra classification still auto-bypasses\n\n* stage 5: SqliteStore pool config in store/mod.rs applies WAL + busy_timeout via after_connect\n\n`store::connect_pool` owns the on-disk SQLite pool config. Every new\nconnection runs `PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL; synchronous = NORMAL;\nbusy_timeout = 5000` from a `SqlitePoolOptions::after_connect` hook,\nso all four pool connections come up identically without callers\nhaving to remember. `InProcessRpc::with_file` now goes through this\nhelper, which keeps WAL where it matters (the codeless data DB on\ndisk) while in-memory test pools — for which WAL is a no-op and the\nsingle-connection harness already pins state — stay on the bare\n`SqlitePool::connect(\"sqlite::memory:\")` path.\n\nA new pool_pragma_tests module proves a fresh on-disk DB reports\njournal_mode=wal, synchronous=1 (NORMAL), busy_timeout=5000, and that\nthe embedded migrator still applies cleanly on top.\n\n* stage 5: stage 5: SqliteStore pool config in crates/codeless-runtime/src/store/mod.rs - apply PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL, PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL, PRAGMA busy_timeout=5000 on every connection acquire via SqlitePoolOptions::after_connect; existing in-memory test stores opt out (WAL is meaningless on :memory:); migrations matrix test still green on a fresh on-disk DB\n\n* stage 7: precheck path tokenizer in diff_verify.rs filters tokens against the diff''s derived prefix set and the worktree-resolve check before the diff-presence check fires; the four real-world false positives (tool.call, rest_proxy.path, metadata_json.delivery.slack, absolute /home/.../ai-runner/Cargo.toml) now drop at the shape filter and stop manufacturing FAILs; verify_handover gains a worktree arg threaded from both call sites (template_runner and scope_patch_emit); unit tests cover the four false positives, real missing paths under a known prefix, unchanged-existing-file via worktree resolve, and absolute/parent-traversal rejection; two pre-existing tests updated to seed a diff-prefix consistent with the new rule\n\n* stage 6: stage 6: precheck path tokenizer in the diff-verify pre-check (grep crates/codeless-runtime for the existing tokenizer; likely in template_runner or a sibling module) - a path-shaped token must EITHER start with a known repo-relative prefix derived from the diff''s file list OR resolve to an existing file under the worktree at check-time; tokens like foo.bar.baz, rest_proxy.path, metadata_json.delivery.slack must NOT be flagged; unit tests cover the four real-world false positives from jobs 01KRX4ZPF and 01KRXH0R plus the negative cases (real missing paths still flagged)\n\n* stage 7: policy_comment threads prior-stage failure block\n\nWiden `auto_bypass_policy::policy_comment` to take an optional\n`PriorFailure { class, detail }` and append a fenced\n`Previous-stage failure: <wire-name>` block (with an optional\n`Detail:` line) after the canned policy paragraph. `None`\nreproduces the pre-widening behaviour byte-for-byte so the bare\ncanned-string callers stay green. Prompt-side detail is normalised\n(trailing whitespace stripped, capped at 400 chars with a single-\nchar U+2026 ellipsis); empty/whitespace-only detail drops the\n`Detail:` line entirely so `OrphanReap` rows do not emit a stub.\n\nUnit tests pin the assembled comment for pre-check-failed +\nreview-fail with details, the bare-policy fall-through, the\nwhitespace-only and exact-ceiling boundaries, and tie\n`failure_class_wire_name` to the serde kebab-case rename via a\n`serde_json` round-trip so a future variant rename trips here\nbefore the prompt drifts from the events stream.\n\nStage 8 will load the prior stage row and pass a `PriorFailure`\nthrough; the single existing callsite in `template_runner.rs`\npasses `None` today.\n\n* stage 7: stage 7: auto_bypass_policy.rs policy_comment - when the prior stage''s failure_class is non-None, append a ''Previous-stage failure: <failure_class>: <failure_detail>'' block to the Operator comment threaded into the next stage''s prompt; the existing canned guidance stays as the lead paragraph; unit test pins the assembled comment for (a) pre-check-failed with detail, (b) review-fail with detail, (c) None failure_class falls through to the bare policy text\n\n* stage 8: bypass-comment-build path threads prior failure_class + failure_detail into policy_comment\n\nWiden classify_stage_failure to take a failure_detail parameter alongside\nfailure_class, build a PriorFailure from the two at the AutoBypass branch,\nand pass it to policy_comment so the threaded fenced block from stage 7\nlands in the next stage''s prompt prefix. The six runtime emit sites each\npass the same detail string they just stamped onto the StageCompleted\nenvelope, so the prompt thread-through and the persisted row stay\nmutually consistent without racing the recorder''s writeback.\n\nIntegration-shaped unit test: seed a Quick-policy job, call\nclassify_stage_failure with PreCheckFailed + a synthetic detail, assert\nthe returned AutoBypass comment starts with the canned policy paragraph\nand ends with the Q4 fenced block carrying the wire-name + detail. A\ncompanion test pins the None failure_class path back to the bare canned\nparagraph byte-for-byte so the existing string-pin contracts elsewhere\nin the runtime still hold.\n\n* stage 8: stage 8: StageRecorder + the bypass-comment-build path - ensure the prior stage''s failure_class + failure_detail are loaded from the stages row at bypass time and passed into policy_comment per stage 7; integration test using MockRunner runs a Failed stage with pre-check-failed + a synthetic detail string, then asserts the next stage''s prompt_prefix carries the threaded block\n\n* stage 9: M-FLOW - bypass thread-through end-to-end - run a templated job under MockRunner where stage 1 fails with pre-check-failed and a fixture detail, stage 2 auto-bypasses, assert the recorded prompt for stage 2 contains both the policy guidance and the prior failure_detail\n\n* stage 9: codeless-types::Event and the list_stages payload\n\nAdd a bypassed: boolean field to the StagesOverview StageData type so\nlater render passes can pick a distinct glyph for a row that was\nadvanced past instead of halted. The flag is derived from\nrollup.stage.bypassed_at on the list_stages seed and preserved across\na stage-started rebuild; the event stream never carries the bypass\ntimestamp.\n\nNo schema change — stages.bypassed_at / bypassed_reason already ride\nthe wire StageRow per wire.ts.\n\n* stage 10: stage 9: codeless-types::Event and the list_stages payload - confirm the bypassed_at + bypassed_reason columns are already on the wire StageRow (they are, per wire.ts line ~2310); add a new field bypassed: boolean to the StagesOverview StageRow TS type derived from rollup.stage.bypassed_at !== null; no schema change needed\n\n* stage 12: stage 10: UI - StagesOverview.tsx stageGlyph() returns a new tone for failed + bypassed: glyph ~ in muted-foreground rather than ! in destructive; stage row title carries an auto-bypassed tooltip plus rail failure_detail; new vitest coverage pins the glyph + tooltip shape\n\n* stage 11: stage 10: UI - StagesOverview.tsx stageGlyph() returns a new tone for failed + bypassed: glyph ~ in muted-foreground rather than ! in destructive; the stage row title carries a tooltip ''auto-bypassed by <policy>: <failure_detail>'' using the new bypassed flag plus the stage''s failureDetail; vitest snapshot updated; no other site touched\n\n* stage 11: UI - JobTimeline chip tooltip + bypass vs hard-fail coverage\n\nThe `stage-auto-bypassed` chip''s tooltip already carries\n`failure_class` + `failure_detail` because the runtime threads both\ninto `comment_used` via `auto_bypass_policy::policy_comment` (stage\n7), so the tooltip `${policy_name}: ${comment_used}` already includes\nthe threaded `Previous-stage failure:` block. No timeline change\nrequired.\n\nNew vitest covers the chip render contract end-to-end:\n\n- bypassed-after-fail: the chip renders with the policy name in the\n  body and a tooltip whose text contains both the wire-name\n  failure_class (`Previous-stage failure: pre-check-failed`) and the\n  verbatim failure_detail (`Detail: scope-patch path drift: …`). Pins\n  the recovery story so a future tooltip composer refactor cannot\n  silently drop either half.\n- hard-fail-no-bypass: a `verify-failed` + `stage-completed{failed}`\n  pair with no `stage-auto-bypassed` envelope produces zero\n  auto-bypass chips — the operator must read this as a halt, not a\n  recovery.\n\nOverview-side `~` glyph + tooltip and gate-panel threaded-comment\ncoverage already lived in `StagesOverview.test.tsx` and\n`ReviewGatePanel.test.tsx`; the timeline was the missing surface.\n\n* stage 12: stage 11: UI - JobTimeline.tsx already renders stage-auto-bypassed via a chip; verify the chip''s tooltip carries the failure_class + failure_detail (extend if not); vitest covers (a) bypassed-after-fail renders ~ in overview + chip in timeline + threaded comment in gate panel and (b) hard-fail-no-bypass still renders ! and no chip\n\n* stage 13: M-UI - UI surfacing end-to-end - load a job page for a completed job whose history includes an auto-bypassed stage; assert StagesOverview shows ~ for the bypassed row, the tooltip carries the policy + failure_detail, JobTimeline shows the chip with the same detail, and StageDetail''s ReviewGatePanel autoBypass block is unchanged\n\n* stage 12: documentation + handover\n\nDOCS/AUTO-BYPASS-DECISIONS.md gains Q8: InfrastructureError shares\nthe Halt branch with caps (Q2); the sqlx primary-code set (8 / 10 /\n11 / 13 / 14), the layering order in classify_stage_failure, why\nNOTADB is carved out, and the StopReason::Infrastructure stamp are\npinned. The Q2 rejection rule is extended to Q8 verbatim and the\ncross-link to Q7 (Relentless does not opt out of Q8) is recorded.\n\nDOCS/JOB-WORKFLOW.md flips the \"TODO precheck rules reference\"\nsection to a documented \"Precheck rules\" section. Rule #1 (Done <->\ndiff cross-check) carries the three-layer path-shape filter\n(looks_path_like shape heuristic, per-run diff-derived prefix\nallow-list, worktree-resolve fallback with absolute / parent-\ntraversal rejection), names the four real-world false positives\nthis job killed, and explicitly defers the fuller diff-verify\noverhaul to a separate job. Rules #2 (section presence) and #3\n(Done for read-only stages) carry forward as numbered rules so a\nprecheck failure can reference precheck rule #N.\n\nCODELESS.md \"What works today\" gains a bullet for the\nauto-bypass-hardening branch listing the four shipped pieces\n(infra-error halt + WAL pool + tokenizer hardening + bypass comment\nthread-through) and the bypassed-vs-hard-fail UI glyph.\n\n* stage 14: stage 12: documentation + handover - DOCS/AUTO-BYPASS-DECISIONS.md gains a new Q for InfrastructureError (the rule + the error-code set + why caps and infra share the Halt branch); DOCS/JOB-WORKFLOW.md precheck-rules TODO section flips to a documented Precheck rule #1 with the tightened tokenizer wording; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' gains a line on infra-error halt + WAL + bypass-comment thread-through + bypassed glyph; final cargo test --workspace + cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings + cargo fmt --check + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green; handover paragraph summarises what is deferred (the precheck tokenizer is hardened, not rewritten - a fuller diff-verify overhaul is a separate job)\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"9011e87c711a6851e20b1e226ce4e5ca4682ad5c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/git/trees/9011e87c711a6851e20b1e226ce4e5ca4682ad5c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/git/commits/a8cb35d4c728fa3aabe61e3179b8c4ac05c86354","verification":{"payload":"tree 9011e87c711a6851e20b1e226ce4e5ca4682ad5c\nparent 9d2474a6405d2f87430991ebecffe2ea8d177384\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1779154246 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1779154246 +0700\n\nauto-bypass-hardening: infra-error halt, WAL pool, precheck tokenizer, threaded bypass comment, ~ glyph (#35)\n\n* stage 1: resolve open questions\n\nFlip the four §\"Open questions\" in\n.codeless/jobs/auto-bypass-hardening/SCOPE.md from open to resolved,\neach with a chosen answer and a *why*. No code lands; this is the\nsingle source of truth the rest of the job cites.\n\n- Q1 (SQLite error codes -> InfrastructureError): five primary codes\n  — 8 READONLY, 10 IOERR, 11 CORRUPT, 13 FULL, 14 CANTOPEN.\n  Explicit exclusion list and the deliberate carve-out for\n  26 NOTADB documented inline.\n- Q2 (WAL pragma timing): per-acquire via after_connect; :memory:\n  skips journal_mode only; busy_timeout=5000 + synchronous=NORMAL\n  apply universally.\n- Q3 (path-shaped tokenizer rule): diff-derived repo-prefix set OR\n  worktree-resolves; self-updating; kills all four motivating-job\n  false positives without touching the diff-presence check.\n- Q4 (bypass-comment thread-through): triple-backtick fence with\n  no language tag, \"Previous-stage failure: <wire-name>\" plus\n  \"Detail: <truncated to 400 Unicode scalars with U+2026 marker>\";\n  None failure_class omits the whole block.\n\nAlso add DOCS/sessions/2026-05-19-auto-bypass-hardening.md as the\nper-stage running log and the stage-2 handover (the new\nFailureClass variant lands in codeless-types::stage and\ncodeless-runtime::store::codec, nothing else).\n\n* stage 0: stage 1: resolve open questions (the four-item set in SCOPE.md - exact SQLite error-code set that maps to InfrastructureError, WAL pragma timing, tokenizer rule for path-shaped tokens, exact wording of the bypass-comment failure_detail thread-through) and inline the resolutions in SCOPE.md; no implementation\n\n* stage 2: codeless-types - add FailureClass::InfrastructureError\n\nAdds the wire variant `infrastructure-error` between `runner-error` and\n`review-patch-invalid`. The variant exists so the auto-bypass policy\n(stage 4) can short-circuit to Halt instead of retrying SQLite\ndisk-full / IOERR / CANTOPEN / CORRUPT / READONLY - a fresh subprocess\non the same host with the same disk would just hit the same wall and\nbury the original cause.\n\n- Variant doc comment explains *why* (operator contract: infra\n  failures halt) per CLAUDE.md R2, not what it stores.\n- failure_class_label / parse_failure_class round-trip in\n  codeless-runtime/src/store/codec.rs.\n- Specta TS snapshots regenerated: codeless-types/tests/wire.ts.snap,\n  codeless-rpc/tests/wire-rpc.ts.snap, and the UI''s\n  ui/codeless-ui/src/lib/rpc/generated/wire.ts (produced by\n  `cargo run -p codeless-rpc --example wire_ts`).\n\nStage 3 (sqlx error -> FailureClass mapper) and stage 4\n(classify_stage_failure halt branch) are deliberately untouched - the\nvariant landing alone is the smallest reverting unit.\n\n* stage 1: stage 2: codeless-types - add FailureClass::InfrastructureError variant with serde wire name infrastructure-error; failure_class_label / parse_failure_class round-trip in codeless-runtime/src/store/codec.rs; specta wire snapshot regenerated; cargo test -p codeless-types green\n\n* stage 3: sqlx error -> FailureClass mapper at RunnerError emit sites\n\nAdd classify_runner_failure_reason and wire it at the three\ntemplate_runner.rs RunnerError emit sites (lines ~1051, 1494, 1579)\nso SQLITE_FULL / IOERR / CORRUPT / CANTOPEN / READONLY classify as\nInfrastructureError per the SCOPE Q1 decision. Primary-code mask\n(extended & 0xff) collapses SQLITE_IOERR_* / SQLITE_CORRUPT_VTAB /\nSQLITE_CANTOPEN_DIRTYWAL onto the right primary. Unit tests pin the\ninfra set, the exclusion set (1/5/6/19/20/21/25/26), extended-code\nmask behavior, non-sqlx reasons, and a sqlx::Error::Database round-\ntrip through Display.\n\n* stage 2: stage 3: codeless-runtime - sqlx error -> FailureClass mapper at every RunnerError emit site (template_runner.rs lines 1051, 1494, 1579 + any others surfaced by grep) so SQLITE_FULL / CANTOPEN / IOERR / CORRUPT classify as InfrastructureError; unit test pins each error code''s classification\n\n* stage 4: classify_stage_failure halts infrastructure-class failures regardless of auto_bypass_policy\n\n`classify_stage_failure` now short-circuits to `FailureAction::Halt`\nwhen the just-classified `FailureClass::InfrastructureError` arrives,\nmirroring the existing `stop_reason.is_some()` short-circuit so a\n`SQLITE_FULL` / `SQLITE_IOERR` / `SQLITE_CORRUPT` / `SQLITE_CANTOPEN`\n/ `SQLITE_READONLY` failure halts the job even under `Relentless`.\nRetrying the same SQL on the same disk would fail the same way; the\noperator must fix the host before the job can advance.\n\nThe halt branch stamps the new `StopReason::Infrastructure` variant\non the job row via `record_infrastructure_halt` (shape mirrors the\nexisting `record_thrash_halt` helper). The UI''s halt-label reader\nnow sees a structural reason instead of falling back to the\ndriver''s generic `RunnerCrash` default.\n\n- `codeless-types::StopReason` grows the `Infrastructure` variant\n  with a doc comment explaining the operator-visible contract.\n- `store/codec.rs` carries the variant on the kebab-case wire name\n  `infrastructure`.\n- `template_runner.rs::classify_stage_failure` widens its signature\n  to take the `Option<FailureClass>` the call site just put on the\n  `StageCompleted{Failed}` envelope; the classifier and the wire\n  event cannot disagree about what tripped.\n- Six call sites updated to pass their statically-known or\n  sqlx-mapped `FailureClass`.\n- Two new unit tests pin the contract: (a) infra-error halts under\n  `Relentless` and stamps `stop_reason`; (b) plain `RunnerError`\n  under the same policy still auto-bypasses and leaves\n  `stop_reason` `None`.\n- Specta wire snapshots (`wire.ts.snap`, `wire-rpc.ts.snap`)\n  regenerated under `SPECTA_UPDATE=1`; bot-core\n  `stop_reason_word` (chat + notify) carries the new variant.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 3: stage 4: classify_stage_failure in template_runner.rs short-circuits to FailureAction::Halt for FailureClass::InfrastructureError regardless of auto_bypass_policy (mirroring the existing stop_reason.is_some() short-circuit); halt writes a structured stop_reason on the job row so the UI labels it Infrastructure failure rather than a generic crash; unit test covers (a) infra-error halts even under Relentless policy and (b) RunnerError without infra classification still auto-bypasses\n\n* stage 5: SqliteStore pool config in store/mod.rs applies WAL + busy_timeout via after_connect\n\n`store::connect_pool` owns the on-disk SQLite pool config. Every new\nconnection runs `PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL; synchronous = NORMAL;\nbusy_timeout = 5000` from a `SqlitePoolOptions::after_connect` hook,\nso all four pool connections come up identically without callers\nhaving to remember. `InProcessRpc::with_file` now goes through this\nhelper, which keeps WAL where it matters (the codeless data DB on\ndisk) while in-memory test pools — for which WAL is a no-op and the\nsingle-connection harness already pins state — stay on the bare\n`SqlitePool::connect(\"sqlite::memory:\")` path.\n\nA new pool_pragma_tests module proves a fresh on-disk DB reports\njournal_mode=wal, synchronous=1 (NORMAL), busy_timeout=5000, and that\nthe embedded migrator still applies cleanly on top.\n\n* stage 5: stage 5: SqliteStore pool config in crates/codeless-runtime/src/store/mod.rs - apply PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL, PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL, PRAGMA busy_timeout=5000 on every connection acquire via SqlitePoolOptions::after_connect; existing in-memory test stores opt out (WAL is meaningless on :memory:); migrations matrix test still green on a fresh on-disk DB\n\n* stage 7: precheck path tokenizer in diff_verify.rs filters tokens against the diff''s derived prefix set and the worktree-resolve check before the diff-presence check fires; the four real-world false positives (tool.call, rest_proxy.path, metadata_json.delivery.slack, absolute /home/.../ai-runner/Cargo.toml) now drop at the shape filter and stop manufacturing FAILs; verify_handover gains a worktree arg threaded from both call sites (template_runner and scope_patch_emit); unit tests cover the four false positives, real missing paths under a known prefix, unchanged-existing-file via worktree resolve, and absolute/parent-traversal rejection; two pre-existing tests updated to seed a diff-prefix consistent with the new rule\n\n* stage 6: stage 6: precheck path tokenizer in the diff-verify pre-check (grep crates/codeless-runtime for the existing tokenizer; likely in template_runner or a sibling module) - a path-shaped token must EITHER start with a known repo-relative prefix derived from the diff''s file list OR resolve to an existing file under the worktree at check-time; tokens like foo.bar.baz, rest_proxy.path, metadata_json.delivery.slack must NOT be flagged; unit tests cover the four real-world false positives from jobs 01KRX4ZPF and 01KRXH0R plus the negative cases (real missing paths still flagged)\n\n* stage 7: policy_comment threads prior-stage failure block\n\nWiden `auto_bypass_policy::policy_comment` to take an optional\n`PriorFailure { class, detail }` and append a fenced\n`Previous-stage failure: <wire-name>` block (with an optional\n`Detail:` line) after the canned policy paragraph. `None`\nreproduces the pre-widening behaviour byte-for-byte so the bare\ncanned-string callers stay green. Prompt-side detail is normalised\n(trailing whitespace stripped, capped at 400 chars with a single-\nchar U+2026 ellipsis); empty/whitespace-only detail drops the\n`Detail:` line entirely so `OrphanReap` rows do not emit a stub.\n\nUnit tests pin the assembled comment for pre-check-failed +\nreview-fail with details, the bare-policy fall-through, the\nwhitespace-only and exact-ceiling boundaries, and tie\n`failure_class_wire_name` to the serde kebab-case rename via a\n`serde_json` round-trip so a future variant rename trips here\nbefore the prompt drifts from the events stream.\n\nStage 8 will load the prior stage row and pass a `PriorFailure`\nthrough; the single existing callsite in `template_runner.rs`\npasses `None` today.\n\n* stage 7: stage 7: auto_bypass_policy.rs policy_comment - when the prior stage''s failure_class is non-None, append a ''Previous-stage failure: <failure_class>: <failure_detail>'' block to the Operator comment threaded into the next stage''s prompt; the existing canned guidance stays as the lead paragraph; unit test pins the assembled comment for (a) pre-check-failed with detail, (b) review-fail with detail, (c) None failure_class falls through to the bare policy text\n\n* stage 8: bypass-comment-build path threads prior failure_class + failure_detail into policy_comment\n\nWiden classify_stage_failure to take a failure_detail parameter alongside\nfailure_class, build a PriorFailure from the two at the AutoBypass branch,\nand pass it to policy_comment so the threaded fenced block from stage 7\nlands in the next stage''s prompt prefix. The six runtime emit sites each\npass the same detail string they just stamped onto the StageCompleted\nenvelope, so the prompt thread-through and the persisted row stay\nmutually consistent without racing the recorder''s writeback.\n\nIntegration-shaped unit test: seed a Quick-policy job, call\nclassify_stage_failure with PreCheckFailed + a synthetic detail, assert\nthe returned AutoBypass comment starts with the canned policy paragraph\nand ends with the Q4 fenced block carrying the wire-name + detail. A\ncompanion test pins the None failure_class path back to the bare canned\nparagraph byte-for-byte so the existing string-pin contracts elsewhere\nin the runtime still hold.\n\n* stage 8: stage 8: StageRecorder + the bypass-comment-build path - ensure the prior stage''s failure_class + failure_detail are loaded from the stages row at bypass time and passed into policy_comment per stage 7; integration test using MockRunner runs a Failed stage with pre-check-failed + a synthetic detail string, then asserts the next stage''s prompt_prefix carries the threaded block\n\n* stage 9: M-FLOW - bypass thread-through end-to-end - run a templated job under MockRunner where stage 1 fails with pre-check-failed and a fixture detail, stage 2 auto-bypasses, assert the recorded prompt for stage 2 contains both the policy guidance and the prior failure_detail\n\n* stage 9: codeless-types::Event and the list_stages payload\n\nAdd a bypassed: boolean field to the StagesOverview StageData type so\nlater render passes can pick a distinct glyph for a row that was\nadvanced past instead of halted. The flag is derived from\nrollup.stage.bypassed_at on the list_stages seed and preserved across\na stage-started rebuild; the event stream never carries the bypass\ntimestamp.\n\nNo schema change — stages.bypassed_at / bypassed_reason already ride\nthe wire StageRow per wire.ts.\n\n* stage 10: stage 9: codeless-types::Event and the list_stages payload - confirm the bypassed_at + bypassed_reason columns are already on the wire StageRow (they are, per wire.ts line ~2310); add a new field bypassed: boolean to the StagesOverview StageRow TS type derived from rollup.stage.bypassed_at !== null; no schema change needed\n\n* stage 12: stage 10: UI - StagesOverview.tsx stageGlyph() returns a new tone for failed + bypassed: glyph ~ in muted-foreground rather than ! in destructive; stage row title carries an auto-bypassed tooltip plus rail failure_detail; new vitest coverage pins the glyph + tooltip shape\n\n* stage 11: stage 10: UI - StagesOverview.tsx stageGlyph() returns a new tone for failed + bypassed: glyph ~ in muted-foreground rather than ! in destructive; the stage row title carries a tooltip ''auto-bypassed by <policy>: <failure_detail>'' using the new bypassed flag plus the stage''s failureDetail; vitest snapshot updated; no other site touched\n\n* stage 11: UI - JobTimeline chip tooltip + bypass vs hard-fail coverage\n\nThe `stage-auto-bypassed` chip''s tooltip already carries\n`failure_class` + `failure_detail` because the runtime threads both\ninto `comment_used` via `auto_bypass_policy::policy_comment` (stage\n7), so the tooltip `${policy_name}: ${comment_used}` already includes\nthe threaded `Previous-stage failure:` block. No timeline change\nrequired.\n\nNew vitest covers the chip render contract end-to-end:\n\n- bypassed-after-fail: the chip renders with the policy name in the\n  body and a tooltip whose text contains both the wire-name\n  failure_class (`Previous-stage failure: pre-check-failed`) and the\n  verbatim failure_detail (`Detail: scope-patch path drift: …`). Pins\n  the recovery story so a future tooltip composer refactor cannot\n  silently drop either half.\n- hard-fail-no-bypass: a `verify-failed` + `stage-completed{failed}`\n  pair with no `stage-auto-bypassed` envelope produces zero\n  auto-bypass chips — the operator must read this as a halt, not a\n  recovery.\n\nOverview-side `~` glyph + tooltip and gate-panel threaded-comment\ncoverage already lived in `StagesOverview.test.tsx` and\n`ReviewGatePanel.test.tsx`; the timeline was the missing surface.\n\n* stage 12: stage 11: UI - JobTimeline.tsx already renders stage-auto-bypassed via a chip; verify the chip''s tooltip carries the failure_class + failure_detail (extend if not); vitest covers (a) bypassed-after-fail renders ~ in overview + chip in timeline + threaded comment in gate panel and (b) hard-fail-no-bypass still renders ! and no chip\n\n* stage 13: M-UI - UI surfacing end-to-end - load a job page for a completed job whose history includes an auto-bypassed stage; assert StagesOverview shows ~ for the bypassed row, the tooltip carries the policy + failure_detail, JobTimeline shows the chip with the same detail, and StageDetail''s ReviewGatePanel autoBypass block is unchanged\n\n* stage 12: documentation + handover\n\nDOCS/AUTO-BYPASS-DECISIONS.md gains Q8: InfrastructureError shares\nthe Halt branch with caps (Q2); the sqlx primary-code set (8 / 10 /\n11 / 13 / 14), the layering order in classify_stage_failure, why\nNOTADB is carved out, and the StopReason::Infrastructure stamp are\npinned. The Q2 rejection rule is extended to Q8 verbatim and the\ncross-link to Q7 (Relentless does not opt out of Q8) is recorded.\n\nDOCS/JOB-WORKFLOW.md flips the \"TODO precheck rules reference\"\nsection to a documented \"Precheck rules\" section. Rule #1 (Done <->\ndiff cross-check) carries the three-layer path-shape filter\n(looks_path_like shape heuristic, per-run diff-derived prefix\nallow-list, worktree-resolve fallback with absolute / parent-\ntraversal rejection), names the four real-world false positives\nthis job killed, and explicitly defers the fuller diff-verify\noverhaul to a separate job. Rules #2 (section presence) and #3\n(Done for read-only stages) carry forward as numbered rules so a\nprecheck failure can reference precheck rule #N.\n\nCODELESS.md \"What works today\" gains a bullet for the\nauto-bypass-hardening branch listing the four shipped pieces\n(infra-error halt + WAL pool + tokenizer hardening + bypass comment\nthread-through) and the bypassed-vs-hard-fail UI glyph.\n\n* stage 14: stage 12: documentation + handover - DOCS/AUTO-BYPASS-DECISIONS.md gains a new Q for InfrastructureError (the rule + the error-code set + why caps and infra share the Halt branch); DOCS/JOB-WORKFLOW.md precheck-rules TODO section flips to a documented Precheck rule #1 with the tightened tokenizer wording; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' gains a line on infra-error halt + WAL + bypass-comment thread-through + bypassed glyph; final cargo test --workspace + cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings + cargo fmt --check + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green; handover paragraph summarises what is deferred (the precheck tokenizer is hardened, not rewritten - a fuller diff-verify overhaul is a separate job)\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqC71GCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAABPIQAJThS9Y84Gnvy8aAW/MmsZaA\nxQ/oETBhojqbmCbHvOvNoJ0TK6jUvYrCtmyUvyU1dLKRJBYoay/RHDTsalZ9DUIM\nm8b2h0i1aWVqdidRYk8Z5C7uONcZCqpihxNpjAcH42Rvf/ymOewXaDbasz7fwGNd\nOEpi8OcXUo9Jah4Yf5lwNFCUQNepB2rqyhZe3r7ZyqdP5Yhn51/wMuEADlB/5QIT\nmN3zEX8FGLamYPXPHho5z3EkEkuZYee7DPe92bPY3o6EEDuVtu588hHjx8Eas0E6\nP9rb48pzhIDFgkkeHZD19PdFNhFHObKt2+5aqoTYWUdXin6MVPyYqM4078Ol+OZD\nL+c7hZHQ/nvYYBNd92t9KhOsiBIin9qfUrIj+uyLdk41pxwc2rzH7VlCt0UsDy4Z\nBmDhmZJhlnkAg4EZxNywq84YeSV5toay4NC2vljicfJW7tcbp1ANmwt7kqlU8YSz\nKkuVUNiwIq2OrtuR9/tBcnxzGf6/3RqnFF+4GNTZ6lW7Sc+/1IkBmYA8EdCTt6oA\nHseAERv5gDNESyGKOAp+tV+pKUnQWQaDkfMme+eTe7NPKBXFQINlaiBs7pGrCP0P\n4TOmP+mH4S/6BF4CN9vXFgVBZiu/hEMd2xMCNGVfPIRzrVnuRTxTFkf1+v7IwmQ7\n4/5R3E7ypM/RxacMgTHH\n=z5zE\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-05-19T01:30:46Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","