l":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/codeless/commit/9918419a90c4b0da50a0c429ef1ef0c89de6ac79","node_id":"C_kwDOSa1B6doAKDk5MTg0MTlhOTBjNGIwZGE1MGEwYzQyOWVmMWVmMGM4OWRlNmFjNzk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/codeless/commit/fa6a2fb877d01865a1030eeb5433fc130a8aaaa7","sha":"fa6a2fb877d01865a1030eeb5433fc130a8aaaa7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/fa6a2fb877d01865a1030eeb5433fc130a8aaaa7"}],"sha":"9918419a90c4b0da50a0c429ef1ef0c89de6ac79","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/9918419a90c4b0da50a0c429ef1ef0c89de6ac79"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/fa6a2fb877d01865a1030eeb5433fc130a8aaaa7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-05-18T22:53:54Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-05-18T22:53:54Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"job-chat: per-Job chat substrate + supervisor agent + pre-armed goals (#34)\n\n* stage 1: resolve open questions\n\nSettle OQ-CHAT-1..5 from DOCS/JOB-CHAT.md inline, in the SCOPE.md\n§Open questions §Settled style, and pin the v0.1 transport set\n(Web/Cli/Telegram/Slack/Supervisor) plus the lowercase-ASCII wire-\nname convention for ChatTransport next to the wire types so adapters\nand SQL share one canonical spelling. Numbering is preserved\n(OQ-CHAT-1..5) for cross-doc citation; updates the stale \"open Q #5\"\ncomment on chat_messages.run_id to reference OQ-CHAT-4.\n\nNo code changes; doc-only stage per the kickoff.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 0: stage 1: resolve open questions (OQ-CHAT-1..5 from JOB-CHAT.md §Open questions; final v0.1 transport set + the wire-name convention for ChatTransport) under SCOPE.md §Open questions and propagate the resolutions into JOB-CHAT.md inline; no implementation\n\n* stage 2: schema — chat_messages + chat_bindings migrations\n\nAdd 0024_chat_messages.sql (chat_messages table, chat_messages_job_idx,\npartial-unique chat_messages_external_idx) and 0025_chat_bindings.sql\n(chat_bindings with thread_id NOT NULL DEFAULT '''' as the no-thread\nsentinel) per DOCS/JOB-CHAT.md §Data model. Row-level comments in both\nfiles explain the NULL-vs-empty-string sentinel choice and why the\nunique constraint on (transport, external_id) is partial.\n\nExtend the migration matrix test to load both new tables: column-order\ncontracts, partial-unique DDL predicate check, NOT NULL + default '''' on\nchat_bindings.thread_id, FKs into jobs, the duplicate-NULL-allowed\nbehaviour the partial index is designed for, and a replay run on a\npopulated DB that proves the new migrations are idempotent.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 1: stage 2: schema — add codeless-runtime/migrations/0024_chat_messages.sql (chat_messages + chat_messages_job_idx + the partial-unique chat_messages_external_idx) and 0025_chat_bindings.sql (chat_bindings with thread_id NOT NULL DEFAULT '''' as the no-thread sentinel), with row-level docs explaining the NULL-vs-empty-string sentinel and why the unique constraint is partial; the existing migration matrix test loads both migrations cleanly on a fresh DB and replay-idempotent on a populated one\n\n* stage 3: wire types in codeless-types — ChatMessage, ChatTransport, ChatRole, ChatBinding, MessageId\n\nAdds the per-Job chat substrate''s wire types from DOCS/JOB-CHAT.md as\npure data in codeless-types so every transport (web, CLI, Telegram,\nSlack, supervisor) and every shell (browser, desktop, iOS, Android)\nshares one definition. The new module sits next to assistant.rs to\nkeep the two CommonChat-renderable surfaces side-by-side. run_id is\ntyped as Option<String> until JOB-WORKFLOW (B) lands its RunId\nnewtype; the wire shape will not change at that point. Snapshot\nregenerated; cargo test -p codeless-types green.\n\n* stage 2: stage 3: wire types in codeless-types (mobile-safe) — ChatMessage, ChatTransport, ChatRole, ChatBinding plus their specta::Type derives; MessageId newtype; specta wire snapshot regenerated; cargo test -p codeless-types green\n\n* stage 4: RPC surface — post_job_message, list_job_messages, bind_chat_thread\n\nAdd the three per-Job chat substrate RPCs (`JOB-CHAT.md` §RPC\nsurface) onto `RpcServer` with ResultEnvelopes lifted from the\nexisting add_repo / list_jobs pattern. Implementations live in a\nnew `codeless-runtime::rpc::job_chat` module backed by a new\n`codeless-runtime::store::chat` module.\n\n`post_job_message` mints the row''s `MessageId` and stamps\n`created_at` server-side so a clock-skewed transport cannot\nreorder the thread. The partial unique index over\n`(transport, external_id)` on telegram / slack rows maps to a\ntyped `Conflict` so adapters skip the duplicate-ingest path\nwithout sniffing error strings.\n\n`list_job_messages` pages newest-first by `created_at` (id\ntiebreaker) with a server-side cap, then reverses the page to\noldest-first so the UI renders top-to-bottom without sorting.\n\n`bind_chat_thread` upserts the binding keyed by\n`(transport, channel_id, thread_id)` with the empty-string\nsentinel matching the PK invariant; the second call against the\nsame key overwrites `bound_at` / `bound_by` so a re-bind is\nidempotent on identity and metadata-fresh.\n\nUnit tests pin (1) the post → list round-trip and field\ndecoding, (2) backward pagination via `before`, (3) the\n`DuplicateExternalId` partial-unique-index conflict path\nincluding the NULL-external-id non-collision case, (4) bind\nupsert idempotency on PK with overwrite semantics, plus\nNotFound paths through the RPC wrapper for every method.\nHTTP routes and `HttpRpcClient` impls wired through.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 3: stage 4: RPC surface — post_job_message, list_job_messages, bind_chat_thread in codeless-rpc::RpcServer with ResultEnvelopes lifted from the existing add_repo / list_jobs pattern; impls in codeless-runtime::rpc::chat backed by a new codeless-runtime::store::chat module; unit tests pin the post → list round-trip, the bind upsert idempotency, and the partial-unique-index conflict path\n\n* stage 5: events on the existing bus — ChatMessageAppended { job_id, message } and ChatBindingCreated { transport, channel_id, thread_id, job_id } on codeless-types::Event with the same kebab-case rename style the surrounding variants use; post_job_message publishes ChatMessageAppended after a successful INSERT (the DuplicateExternalId branch returns Conflict without an event so a redelivered Telegram inbound does not double-fan-out) and bind_chat_thread publishes ChatBindingCreated after the upsert; StageRecorder gains no responsibility — chat keeps its own write path; specta wire snapshot regenerated; integration test asserts a single ChatMessageAppended per post call and that aborting/respawning the recorder neither mutates chat_messages nor publishes extra chat events\n\n* stage 5: M-C1-A — chat substrate compiles end-to-end: migrations land, wire types regen clean, RPCs pin behaviour, events fire on insert; no transport-side code yet\n\n* stage 6: Web UI — ChatTab.tsx rewritten over the new job-chat RPCs\n\n* `ui/codeless-ui/src/modules/jobs/ChatTab.tsx` is the single web\n  entry point onto the JOB-CHAT.md substrate. No transport-local\n  message store survives here — every rendered row lives in\n  `chat_messages` on the server and every send goes through\n  `post_job_message`. The legacy `JobChat` in `RunPane.tsx` (which\n  parsed `CHAT.md` out of the worktree and kept an optimistic local\n  list) is incompatible with the multi-transport invariant and is\n  not used by the new tab.\n\n* SWR-style cache lives module-scoped in `ChatTab.tsx`, keyed on\n  `job_id`. On the first mount that names a given Job, the cache\n  rehydrates from `list_job_messages` (limit 200, newest-first\n  paging); subsequent mounts reuse the warm entry so re-opening the\n  tab does not blank the conversation. Subsequent rows arrive\n  exclusively via the `chat-message-appended` event from the\n  existing `useEventStream` subscription pool; the local post path\n  does not optimistically append because the server is the single\n  source of truth for ordering. `MessageId` is the dedup gate\n  (Set<MessageId>) so a redelivered envelope (server restart,\n  multi-tab race, SSE replay) never double-renders.\n\n* `crates/codeless-rpc/examples/wire_ts.rs` now registers the chat\n  wire surface (`ChatMessage`, `ChatBinding`, `ChatTransport`,\n  `ChatRole`, `MessageId`, `PostJobMessageArgs`,\n  `ListJobMessagesArgs`, `ListJobMessagesResult`,\n  `BindChatThreadArgs`) so the UI-side `generated/wire.ts` is\n  regenerated to include the types — the codeless-types and\n  codeless-rpc snapshot tests already pinned them, but the\n  combined exporter that the UI consumes had not picked them up.\n  `RpcMethodMap` in `lib/rpc/methods.ts` gains\n  `post_job_message`, `list_job_messages`, and `bind_chat_thread`\n  entries so every typed call site compiles against the same\n  shape.\n\n* New vitest `ChatTab.test.tsx` adds a fixture `RpcClient` that\n  records calls, fabricates the `chat-message-appended` envelope\n  the runtime would have published after the INSERT, and exposes\n  `publish()` so a test can drive a non-origin (Telegram)\n  fan-out by hand. Four cases cover the load-bearing claims:\n  rehydrate-on-mount, the\n  chat_tab_round_trips_a_post round-trip (input →\n  `post_job_message` → `ChatMessageAppended` → render), a\n  non-origin fan-out without re-fetch, and `MessageId` dedup on a\n  redelivered envelope. `pnpm vitest run` green across all 24\n  files / 122 tests; `cargo check --workspace` green; both specta\n  snapshot tests green.\n\n* stage 6: stage 6: Web UI — rewrite ui/codeless-ui/src/modules/jobs/ChatTab.tsx over the new RPCs; remove the existing transport-local message store (whatever shape it has today) and replace its in-memory list with a SWR-style cache keyed on job_id that rehydrates from list_job_messages on mount and appends from ChatMessageAppended on the SSE stream; pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green; the new vitest chat_tab_round_trips_a_post adds a fixture RpcClient and asserts the message input → post_job_message → ChatMessageAppended → render loop without touching the network\n\n* stage 7: Telegram adapter — chat forwarder + bind + delivery receipts\n\nAdds the chat-substrate inbound/outbound paths for Telegram from\nJOB-CHAT.md \"Transport adapters\":\n\n  - inbound long-poll now mirrors every text update in a bound\n    channel into chat_messages via post_job_message\n    (transport=telegram, external_id=<platform message_id>); the\n    partial unique index handles Telegram''s at-least-once redelivery\n    as a typed Conflict that the adapter drops silently.\n  - new inbound_chat module owns `/codeless bind <job_id>` and\n    writes the (transport, channel, thread) -> job_id row via\n    bind_chat_thread; the existing command dispatcher\n    (/status, /stop, /resume) keeps working in parallel.\n  - new chat module owns the outbound ChatMessageAppended\n    subscriber: skips Telegram-origin rows (echo suppression),\n    skips rows whose metadata.delivery.telegram is already set\n    (presence-based idempotency), fans every other row to every\n    Telegram binding for the Job, then records the delivery\n    receipt.\n\nSubstrate additions to support the forwarder:\n\n  - SqliteStore::update_chat_message_delivery — read-merge-write\n    inside a transaction; only metadata_json is touched, never\n    body or external_id (immutability bias).\n  - SqliteStore::list_chat_bindings_for_job — reverse lookup used\n    by every outbound forwarder.\n  - RpcServer::update_chat_message_delivery,\n    list_chat_bindings_for_job, get_chat_binding — minimal new\n    surface area, wired through http server + client + specta\n    wire snapshot.\n\nIntegration tests in crates/codeless-telegram/tests/chat_forwarder.rs\nstand up a real InProcessRpc + a wiremock-backed Telegram API and\nexercise the three behaviour bullets: web-origin → forwarded +\nreceipt written, telegram-origin → not echoed, prior receipt →\nnot re-forwarded.\n\n* stage 7: stage 7: Telegram adapter — codeless-telegram inbound writes via post_job_message with transport=''telegram'' and the platform message id as external_id; outbound subscribes to ChatMessageAppended and forwards every non-telegram-origin message via the Bot API, then writes the delivery receipt to chat_messages.metadata_json.delivery.telegram via a new update_chat_message_delivery store method (no UPDATE on body / external_id ever, per JOB-CHAT.md immutability bias); /codeless bind <job_id> writes to chat_bindings; idempotency is presence-based on metadata.delivery.telegram\n\n* stage 8: cold-load + echo-suppression — on /codeless bind, the Telegram adapter calls list_job_messages with the small N-tail and posts a single condensed ''joining mid-thread'' summary; the asymmetric echo-suppression rule moves into a shared helper in codeless-bot-core (origin-transport skip, non-origin forward + receipt), with bot_chat_e2e::origin_transport_skips_self_post and bot_chat_e2e::cross_transport_forwards_with_receipt as the load-bearing tests against an in-memory bus + a CannedTelegramApi\n\n* stage 8: stage 8: cold-load + echo-suppression — on /codeless bind, the Telegram adapter calls list_job_messages with the small N-tail and posts a single condensed ''joining mid-thread'' summary; the asymmetric echo-suppression rule moves into a shared helper in codeless-bot-core (origin-transport skip, non-origin forward + receipt), with bot_chat_e2e::origin_transport_skips_self_post and bot_chat_e2e::cross_transport_forwards_with_receipt as the load-bearing tests against an in-memory bus + a CannedTelegramApi\n\n* stage 9: M-C1-B — C1 end-to-end: a message typed in the web UI CHAT tab appears in a bound Telegram thread within one event tick (and vice versa); the punch list in JOB-CHAT.md §C1 punch-list is fully ticked off; JOB-CHAT.md status updated to record C1 as shipped; no supervisor yet\n\n* stage 9: supervisor module scaffold — codeless-runtime::supervisor\n\nJOB-CHAT.md (C2) stage 9. New `codeless-runtime::supervisor` module\n(not a crate; satisfies R1 by construction). `drive_job` spawns one\nsupervisor task per Run after the row transitions to `Running` and the\n`JobStarted` event is published; the task self-terminates on a Run\nterminal event (`JobCompleted` / `JobFailed` / `JobStopped`).\n`AwaitingReview` is non-terminal so the same conversation thread spans\na review gate; a fresh Run spawns a fresh supervisor.\n\nVoice contract: the supervisor''s only outbound channel is\n`post_job_message` with `transport=''supervisor''`. The module ships\nwith a `cargo test`-time grep that fails if any forbidden substring\n(stdlib print macros, loud tracing levels, direct event-bus publish)\nre-appears in the module source. The forbidden tokens are constructed\nfrom string fragments so the linter does not trip itself.\n\nTests:\n- `codeless_runtime::supervisor::lint::supervisor_module_source_has_no_forbidden_outbound_calls`\n- `codeless_runtime::supervisor::tests::{exits_on_job_completed, exits_on_job_failed, exits_on_job_stopped, stays_alive_through_non_terminal_events}`\n- `supervisor_e2e::supervisor_spawns_on_run_start_and_exits_on_run_terminal`\n- `supervisor_e2e::each_run_spawns_an_independent_supervisor`\n\n* stage 10: stage 9: supervisor module scaffold — codeless-runtime::supervisor (module, not crate; R1 by construction); spawned by drive_job when the Run enters Running; subscribed to ChatMessageAppended filtered to its job_id; cancelled on Run terminal status (Completed / Failed / Stopped / AwaitingReview is not terminal); a fresh Run spawns a fresh supervisor; supervisor_e2e::supervisor_spawns_on_run_start_and_exits_on_run_terminal green; the supervisor''s only outbound voice is post_job_message with transport=''supervisor'', which is enforced by grep + a clippy-style lint rule in the module''s mod.rs (no eprintln, no tracing::info that surfaces to user, no direct event publish)\n\n* stage 10: supervisor tool surface (read-only) — get_job_state, read_events, read_handover, read_template, read_stage_log, read_notes; plus the single write tool post_chat_message; each tool routes through existing RPCs / store reads so the supervisor never imports tokio::process or std::process (enforced by grep test in supervisor/mod.rs); supervisor_e2e::supervisor_answers_what_stage_is_it_on green using the mock runner + canned event timeline\n\nThe supervisor module now exposes a typed Tools struct alongside\nits lifecycle reactor. Tools owns Arc<EventBus> + Arc<SqliteStore>\nand an optional worktree_root for the filesystem-backed reads.\n\nTool surface\n  - get_job_state — store.get_job + list_stages_for_job, projects a\n    JobStateView { status, started_at, current_stage, stage_count }\n    where current_stage is the highest-ordinal Running row falling\n    back to the highest-ordinal row overall.\n  - read_events — bus.fetch_recent_for_job, a new pub method on\n    EventBus that pulls the events table tail for one job in cursor-\n    ascending order, clamped to READ_EVENTS_LIMIT_MAX = 500.\n  - read_handover — tokio::fs::read_to_string on the conventional\n    runs/<job_id>/<stage_id>/handover.md path via the existing\n    handover::handover_path helper.\n  - read_template — job row''s template_yaml column. None for prompt-\n    only jobs.\n  - read_stage_log — runs/<job_id>/<stage_id>/log.md.\n  - read_notes — collects runs/<job_id>/notes/*.md sorted by filename.\n  - post_chat_message — the supervisor''s only voice. Inserts a row\n    with transport=Supervisor, role=Assistant, author=\"supervisor\",\n    then publishes Event::ChatMessageAppended. Mirrors the contract\n    of rpc::job_chat::post_job_message.\n\nReactor\n  spawn_supervisor_with_tools(bus, store, job_id) wires the C2\n  question/answer loop: on a non-supervisor ChatMessageAppended whose\n  body contains \"what stage\" / \"which stage\", call get_job_state and\n  reply via post_chat_message. The original spawn_supervisor entry\n  point keeps its bus-only signature for the existing driver wiring\n  and lifecycle tests.\n\nLint\n  The grep in supervisor::lint now also rejects literal mentions of\n  the stdlib process module and tokio''s process module — keeping R1\n  of CLAUDE.md and Hard rule 2 of JOB-CHAT.md grep-enforced. Doc-\n  comments avoid the forbidden literals via word-wrap so the lint\n  source itself stays clean.\n\nTests\n  - supervisor::tools::tests pin get_job_state''s \"pick Running stage\"\n    rule and post_chat_message''s insert + publish shape.\n  - supervisor_e2e::supervisor_answers_what_stage_is_it_on stands up\n    a job, inserts a Stage row at ordinal 10, posts a \"what stage is\n    it on?\" message via the Web transport, then asserts a Supervisor\n    ChatMessageAppended envelope arrives within 2s whose body cites\n    \"stage 10\". The canned event timeline is the inserted Stage row +\n    the Web-origin chat post; no live mock runner is needed because\n    the tool surface reads from SQLite, not from the live event tail.\n\ncargo test -p codeless-runtime --lib supervisor green (7/7).\ncargo test -p codeless-runtime --test supervisor_e2e green (3/3).\ncargo clippy -p codeless-runtime --all-targets -- -D warnings clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 11: stage 10: supervisor tool surface (read-only) — get_job_state, read_events, read_handover, read_template, read_stage_log, read_notes; plus the single write tool post_chat_message; each tool routes through existing RPCs / store reads so the supervisor never imports tokio::process or std::process (enforced by grep test in supervisor/mod.rs); supervisor_e2e::supervisor_answers_what_stage_is_it_on green using the mock runner + canned event timeline\n\n* stage 11: supervisor model + prompt — supervisor/prompt.rs holds the system prompt + per-tool descriptions as reviewer-readable text (kept under 4 KB so a typical Haiku reply stays under the 2c-per-turn budget) and the deterministic format_terminal_summary helper; supervisor/claude.rs wires a one-shot ClaudeRunnerAdapter behind the new supervisor-claude cargo feature, never naming std::process or tokio::process so R1 + the lint test still hold; on Run terminal envelope the supervisor''s run_with_tools loop reads the stage list (including failure_detail) via tools.store_arc() and posts a one-paragraph summary citing each stage name before exiting; driver.rs now spawns the tool-equipped supervisor so the summary fires in production; supervisor_e2e::supervisor_posts_terminal_summary_on_run_failed green\n\n* stage 12: stage 11: supervisor model + prompt — wire the supervisor''s Claude session via the existing claude runner under a host-only feature flag; system prompt + tool descriptions live in supervisor/prompt.rs so they are reviewable as text; on Run terminal status the supervisor posts a one-paragraph summary referencing the stage names and visible failure_detail then exits; the prompt is short enough that a typical reply costs <2c at current pricing\n\n* stage 13: M-C2 — C2 end-to-end: with a Run in Running state, typing what-stage-is-it-on in the web CHAT tab gets a real supervisor reply within a few seconds that references the current stage name and the most recent ai-token; supervisor_e2e is green; JOB-CHAT.md status updated to record C2 as shipped\n\n* stage 12: action tools — stop_job and add_job_note in supervisor/tools/actions.rs; both route through the existing SqliteStore + EventBus surfaces so the resulting JobStopped envelope is byte-identical to the UI [stop] button''s and the ChatMessageAppended row carries the same shape every transport renders; the events row has no actor column (the table predates the concept), so the per-action provenance lives on the chat row''s transport=''supervisor''; ad-hoc preview window is a 5-second tokio::time::sleep with a cancellation check on a fresh ChatMessageAppended from a user role containing /^wait\\b/i, with the post-action summary / cancellation row''s metadata.resolves pointing back at the preview row id; supervisor_e2e::ad_hoc_stop_aborts_on_user_wait and supervisor_e2e::ad_hoc_stop_fires_after_window green\n\n* stage 14: stage 12: action tools — stop_job and add_job_note in supervisor/tools/actions.rs; both route through existing RPCs and emit the same JobCancelled / JobNoteAdded events the UI buttons produce, with the events row''s actor=''supervisor'' so the audit trail tells you which surface triggered; ad-hoc preview window is a 5-second tokio::time::sleep with a cancellation check on a fresh ChatMessageAppended from a user role containing /^wait\\b/i; supervisor_e2e::ad_hoc_stop_aborts_on_user_wait and supervisor_e2e::ad_hoc_stop_fires_after_window green\n\n* stage 13: supervisor_goals migration + store\n\nAdd migrations/0026_supervisor_goals.sql with the closed kind set\n(deadline-stop / threshold-stop / event-notify), typed condition /\naction payloads validated at write time, and the armed → fired |\ncancelled | superseded status walk JOB-CHAT.md §C3 specifies. The\npartial index on (run_id, created_at) WHERE status=''armed'' is what\nkeeps supervisor-boot rehydration cheap as terminal rows accumulate\nacross a Run''s lifetime.\n\nstore::supervisor_goals exposes insert_goal / list_armed_for_run /\nmark_fired / mark_cancelled / mark_superseded. Validation is in Rust\n(SQLite has no native enum) and runs through SupervisorGoalKind /\nGoalCondition / GoalAction; mismatched kind/condition triples cannot\nland on disk, and a row written with the wrong serialised shape\nsurfaces a typed decode error rather than a silent no-op. The\nmark_* helpers are idempotent — second-call against a terminal row\nreturns MarkOutcome::NoChange — so a deadline arm racing with a\nuser cancel can lose cleanly.\n\npause_after_stage rides in GoalAction; round-trips through insert /\nlist, surfaces ExecutionState::NoOpFailed so the supervisor reactor\ncan short-circuit without invoking a runner-side pause. JOB-WORKFLOW\n(A.5) is the affordance that flips this to Ready.\n\n* stage 15: stage 13: supervisor_goals migration + store — codeless-runtime/migrations/0026_supervisor_goals.sql with the columns from JOB-CHAT.md §C3 (id, run_id, kind, condition_json, action_json, authorised_by, status, created_at, fired_at); codeless-runtime::store::supervisor_goals with insert_goal / list_armed_for_run / mark_fired / mark_cancelled / mark_superseded; goal_kinds in v0.1 = deadline-stop, threshold-stop, event-notify; condition_json and action_json validated against a typed enum at write time; pause_after_stage is parsed but produces a structured Failed (no-op until JOB-WORKFLOW (A.5))\n\n* stage 14: pre-armed action loop — supervisor watches its goals via a select! loop combining the event bus, a deadline timer, and a tokio::time::sleep_until per armed goal; on fire, the action invokes immediately (no preview, per Hard rule 4 of JOB-CHAT.md) and the supervisor posts the ''I just did X because Y'' summary as a chat row referencing the authorising chat_messages.id; supervisor_e2e::deadline_stop_fires_at_t_plus_one_hour green using a tokio time-driver pause + advance\n\nWires the pre-armed firing path inside codeless-runtime/src/supervisor/mod.rs: on boot the supervisor rehydrates armed supervisor_goals rows via list_armed_for_run and pushes one Pin<Box<dyn Future>> per deadline-stop goal onto a FuturesUnordered (threshold-stop / event-notify variants stay out of scope until their respective metric / event sources land). The reactor''s select! loop now races the existing event-bus stream against the timer set; the goals branch carries an `if !timers.is_empty()` guard so an empty FuturesUnordered does not hot-spin the select.\n\nfire_goal owns the race against a concurrent user cancellation: it calls Tools::mark_goal_fired first (the SqliteStore''s UPDATE … WHERE status=''armed'' serialises the writers) and only invokes the action when the transition lands. The Tools surface gains fire_pre_armed_goal and mark_goal_fired so the reactor''s seam to persistence stays single-source — the supervisor module never touches the store module directly. The post-action chat row carries metadata.replies_to = authorised_by and metadata.goal_id = goal.id so the audit trail JOB-CHAT.md Hard rule 4 promises is a foreign-key edge readable through the normal list_chat_messages surface.\n\ndeadline arming: arm_goal_timer stores absolute wall-clock deadlines (deadline_ms - now_ms() at boot) and the per-arm sleep is a tokio::time::sleep over that delta, so the production path waits on real wall time and the test path drives mock tokio time. A past deadline yields a zero-duration sleep so a restart that lands after the original deadline still catches the missed fire on the first select tick.\n\nsupervisor_e2e::deadline_stop_fires_at_t_plus_one_hour pauses tokio time *after* spawning the supervisor (so subscribe_since and list_armed_for_run go through sqlx without the paused timer-driver starving the pool), advances 3_600_001ms past the deadline, then resumes time so the fire flow''s sqlx hops (mark_fired / update_job / insert_chat_message) and broadcast wake-ups complete. The test asserts: a) the post-fire summary references the authorising MessageId and the goal id in metadata, b) no System-role preview row was emitted (pre-armed actions skip the preview window, per Hard rule 4''s second regime), c) the job row transitions to Stopped and the goal leaves `armed`. tokio dev-deps gain the test-util feature for time::pause + time::advance.\n\n* stage 16: stage 14: pre-armed action loop — supervisor watches its goals via a select! loop combining the event bus, a deadline timer, and a tokio::time::sleep_until per armed goal; on fire, the action invokes immediately (no preview, per Hard rule 4 of JOB-CHAT.md) and the supervisor posts the ''I just did X because Y'' summary as a chat row referencing the authorising chat_messages.id; supervisor_e2e::deadline_stop_fires_at_t_plus_one_hour green using a tokio time-driver pause + advance\n\n* stage 15: goal rehydration on supervisor boot — `run_with_tools` scans `armed` rows for its `run_id` on every spawn (including a fresh process after a server bounce), re-arms a `tokio::time::sleep` per deadline-stop row, and walks rows whose condition can no longer trip to `superseded` with a `transport=''supervisor''` chat note quoting the reason; threshold-stop / event-notify rows stay armed pending their signal-source wiring; tests `supervisor_e2e::supervisor_rehydrates_deadline_after_restart` and `supervisor_e2e::supervisor_supersedes_armed_goals_when_run_is_terminal_at_boot` green — the rehydrate test drops the first supervisor mid-Run with `abort()`, spawns a fresh one, then `tokio::time::pause`/`advance`s past the deadline to assert the goal still fires with the same authorising-chat-id metadata edge\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 17: stage 15: goal rehydration on supervisor boot — a supervisor that starts (or restarts after a server bounce mid-Run) scans armed rows for its run_id, re-arms timers / event watchers, marks rows whose condition no longer makes sense as Superseded with the reason; supervisor_e2e::supervisor_rehydrates_deadline_after_restart green (drop the supervisor mid-run, spawn a fresh one, advance time, assert the goal still fires)\n\n* stage 16: Slack adapter parity — new codeless-slack chat.rs forwarder mirrors codeless-telegram::chat one-for-one against the Slack Web API; ChatTransport::Slack rows in chat_bindings drive fan-out, ChatPoster::post returns the parent ts that lands as metadata_json.delivery.slack via update_chat_message_delivery; the shared echo-suppression decision lives in codeless_bot_core::chat_forward::classify so adding the second transport is a one-line caller change rather than a re-derived rule; SlackBot::spawn_with always wires a ChatForwarder (drained in shutdown alongside the outbound failure-card publisher); slack_chat_e2e mirrors bot_chat_e2e against a CannedSlackApi wiremock with cross_transport_forwards_with_receipt and origin_transport_skips_self_post green\n\n* stage 18: stage 16: Slack adapter parity — new codeless-slack crate following the codeless-telegram shape; same chat_bindings.transport=''slack'' rows; the shared echo-suppression + outbound-receipt helper in codeless-bot-core is the only thing that needs to know two transports exist; slack_chat_e2e mirrors bot_chat_e2e against a CannedSlackApi\n\n* stage 17: documentation + handover — JOB-CHAT.md Status rows for C1, C2, C3 record the per-Job chat substrate, supervisor module, and pre-armed-goals work landed on codeless/job-chat with the load-bearing test names; resolved-OQ updates already inline since stage 1; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' lines for the three landed surfaces (unified chat table, read-only supervisor, action tools + supervisor_goals); migration-list test grew supervisor_goals so the appendix-A invariant matches the actual schema; cargo test --workspace, cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo fmt --check, pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint, and pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test all green\n\nDeferred (not in this job''s scope): mcp_forward parity from PLUGIN-MCP is unrelated and stays in its own plugin track; mobile-shell wiring of the new chat surfaces is Phase 6, since codeless-tauri-mobile only sees codeless-types and codeless-client and the substrate already meets that boundary; multi-user trust beyond the chat_bindings.bound_by operator (per-user OIDC + per-action ACLs) is Phase 7 OIDC per OQ-CHAT-3.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 20: stage 17: documentation + handover — JOB-CHAT.md Status rows for C1, C2, C3; resolved-OQ updates inline; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' lines for the three landed surfaces; final cargo test --workspace + cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings + cargo fmt --check + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green; one paragraph in the job handover summarising what''s deferred (mcp_forward parity from PLUGIN-MCP is unrelated; mobile-shell wiring of chat surfaces is Phase 6; multi-user trust is Phase 7 OIDC)\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"4f2d30a19ff61ff00a32bac50061de0fd5edd45e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/git/trees/4f2d30a19ff61ff00a32bac50061de0fd5edd45e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/git/commits/fa6a2fb877d01865a1030eeb5433fc130a8aaaa7","verification":{"payload":"tree 4f2d30a19ff61ff00a32bac50061de0fd5edd45e\nparent 6d8ab52604fda32d2c38d5a4e12447a679cfaaf1\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1779144834 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1779144834 +0700\n\njob-chat: per-Job chat substrate + supervisor agent + pre-armed goals (#34)\n\n* stage 1: resolve open questions\n\nSettle OQ-CHAT-1..5 from DOCS/JOB-CHAT.md inline, in the SCOPE.md\n§Open questions §Settled style, and pin the v0.1 transport set\n(Web/Cli/Telegram/Slack/Supervisor) plus the lowercase-ASCII wire-\nname convention for ChatTransport next to the wire types so adapters\nand SQL share one canonical spelling. Numbering is preserved\n(OQ-CHAT-1..5) for cross-doc citation; updates the stale \"open Q #5\"\ncomment on chat_messages.run_id to reference OQ-CHAT-4.\n\nNo code changes; doc-only stage per the kickoff.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 0: stage 1: resolve open questions (OQ-CHAT-1..5 from JOB-CHAT.md §Open questions; final v0.1 transport set + the wire-name convention for ChatTransport) under SCOPE.md §Open questions and propagate the resolutions into JOB-CHAT.md inline; no implementation\n\n* stage 2: schema — chat_messages + chat_bindings migrations\n\nAdd 0024_chat_messages.sql (chat_messages table, chat_messages_job_idx,\npartial-unique chat_messages_external_idx) and 0025_chat_bindings.sql\n(chat_bindings with thread_id NOT NULL DEFAULT '''' as the no-thread\nsentinel) per DOCS/JOB-CHAT.md §Data model. Row-level comments in both\nfiles explain the NULL-vs-empty-string sentinel choice and why the\nunique constraint on (transport, external_id) is partial.\n\nExtend the migration matrix test to load both new tables: column-order\ncontracts, partial-unique DDL predicate check, NOT NULL + default '''' on\nchat_bindings.thread_id, FKs into jobs, the duplicate-NULL-allowed\nbehaviour the partial index is designed for, and a replay run on a\npopulated DB that proves the new migrations are idempotent.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 1: stage 2: schema — add codeless-runtime/migrations/0024_chat_messages.sql (chat_messages + chat_messages_job_idx + the partial-unique chat_messages_external_idx) and 0025_chat_bindings.sql (chat_bindings with thread_id NOT NULL DEFAULT '''' as the no-thread sentinel), with row-level docs explaining the NULL-vs-empty-string sentinel and why the unique constraint is partial; the existing migration matrix test loads both migrations cleanly on a fresh DB and replay-idempotent on a populated one\n\n* stage 3: wire types in codeless-types — ChatMessage, ChatTransport, ChatRole, ChatBinding, MessageId\n\nAdds the per-Job chat substrate''s wire types from DOCS/JOB-CHAT.md as\npure data in codeless-types so every transport (web, CLI, Telegram,\nSlack, supervisor) and every shell (browser, desktop, iOS, Android)\nshares one definition. The new module sits next to assistant.rs to\nkeep the two CommonChat-renderable surfaces side-by-side. run_id is\ntyped as Option<String> until JOB-WORKFLOW (B) lands its RunId\nnewtype; the wire shape will not change at that point. Snapshot\nregenerated; cargo test -p codeless-types green.\n\n* stage 2: stage 3: wire types in codeless-types (mobile-safe) — ChatMessage, ChatTransport, ChatRole, ChatBinding plus their specta::Type derives; MessageId newtype; specta wire snapshot regenerated; cargo test -p codeless-types green\n\n* stage 4: RPC surface — post_job_message, list_job_messages, bind_chat_thread\n\nAdd the three per-Job chat substrate RPCs (`JOB-CHAT.md` §RPC\nsurface) onto `RpcServer` with ResultEnvelopes lifted from the\nexisting add_repo / list_jobs pattern. Implementations live in a\nnew `codeless-runtime::rpc::job_chat` module backed by a new\n`codeless-runtime::store::chat` module.\n\n`post_job_message` mints the row''s `MessageId` and stamps\n`created_at` server-side so a clock-skewed transport cannot\nreorder the thread. The partial unique index over\n`(transport, external_id)` on telegram / slack rows maps to a\ntyped `Conflict` so adapters skip the duplicate-ingest path\nwithout sniffing error strings.\n\n`list_job_messages` pages newest-first by `created_at` (id\ntiebreaker) with a server-side cap, then reverses the page to\noldest-first so the UI renders top-to-bottom without sorting.\n\n`bind_chat_thread` upserts the binding keyed by\n`(transport, channel_id, thread_id)` with the empty-string\nsentinel matching the PK invariant; the second call against the\nsame key overwrites `bound_at` / `bound_by` so a re-bind is\nidempotent on identity and metadata-fresh.\n\nUnit tests pin (1) the post → list round-trip and field\ndecoding, (2) backward pagination via `before`, (3) the\n`DuplicateExternalId` partial-unique-index conflict path\nincluding the NULL-external-id non-collision case, (4) bind\nupsert idempotency on PK with overwrite semantics, plus\nNotFound paths through the RPC wrapper for every method.\nHTTP routes and `HttpRpcClient` impls wired through.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 3: stage 4: RPC surface — post_job_message, list_job_messages, bind_chat_thread in codeless-rpc::RpcServer with ResultEnvelopes lifted from the existing add_repo / list_jobs pattern; impls in codeless-runtime::rpc::chat backed by a new codeless-runtime::store::chat module; unit tests pin the post → list round-trip, the bind upsert idempotency, and the partial-unique-index conflict path\n\n* stage 5: events on the existing bus — ChatMessageAppended { job_id, message } and ChatBindingCreated { transport, channel_id, thread_id, job_id } on codeless-types::Event with the same kebab-case rename style the surrounding variants use; post_job_message publishes ChatMessageAppended after a successful INSERT (the DuplicateExternalId branch returns Conflict without an event so a redelivered Telegram inbound does not double-fan-out) and bind_chat_thread publishes ChatBindingCreated after the upsert; StageRecorder gains no responsibility — chat keeps its own write path; specta wire snapshot regenerated; integration test asserts a single ChatMessageAppended per post call and that aborting/respawning the recorder neither mutates chat_messages nor publishes extra chat events\n\n* stage 5: M-C1-A — chat substrate compiles end-to-end: migrations land, wire types regen clean, RPCs pin behaviour, events fire on insert; no transport-side code yet\n\n* stage 6: Web UI — ChatTab.tsx rewritten over the new job-chat RPCs\n\n* `ui/codeless-ui/src/modules/jobs/ChatTab.tsx` is the single web\n  entry point onto the JOB-CHAT.md substrate. No transport-local\n  message store survives here — every rendered row lives in\n  `chat_messages` on the server and every send goes through\n  `post_job_message`. The legacy `JobChat` in `RunPane.tsx` (which\n  parsed `CHAT.md` out of the worktree and kept an optimistic local\n  list) is incompatible with the multi-transport invariant and is\n  not used by the new tab.\n\n* SWR-style cache lives module-scoped in `ChatTab.tsx`, keyed on\n  `job_id`. On the first mount that names a given Job, the cache\n  rehydrates from `list_job_messages` (limit 200, newest-first\n  paging); subsequent mounts reuse the warm entry so re-opening the\n  tab does not blank the conversation. Subsequent rows arrive\n  exclusively via the `chat-message-appended` event from the\n  existing `useEventStream` subscription pool; the local post path\n  does not optimistically append because the server is the single\n  source of truth for ordering. `MessageId` is the dedup gate\n  (Set<MessageId>) so a redelivered envelope (server restart,\n  multi-tab race, SSE replay) never double-renders.\n\n* `crates/codeless-rpc/examples/wire_ts.rs` now registers the chat\n  wire surface (`ChatMessage`, `ChatBinding`, `ChatTransport`,\n  `ChatRole`, `MessageId`, `PostJobMessageArgs`,\n  `ListJobMessagesArgs`, `ListJobMessagesResult`,\n  `BindChatThreadArgs`) so the UI-side `generated/wire.ts` is\n  regenerated to include the types — the codeless-types and\n  codeless-rpc snapshot tests already pinned them, but the\n  combined exporter that the UI consumes had not picked them up.\n  `RpcMethodMap` in `lib/rpc/methods.ts` gains\n  `post_job_message`, `list_job_messages`, and `bind_chat_thread`\n  entries so every typed call site compiles against the same\n  shape.\n\n* New vitest `ChatTab.test.tsx` adds a fixture `RpcClient` that\n  records calls, fabricates the `chat-message-appended` envelope\n  the runtime would have published after the INSERT, and exposes\n  `publish()` so a test can drive a non-origin (Telegram)\n  fan-out by hand. Four cases cover the load-bearing claims:\n  rehydrate-on-mount, the\n  chat_tab_round_trips_a_post round-trip (input →\n  `post_job_message` → `ChatMessageAppended` → render), a\n  non-origin fan-out without re-fetch, and `MessageId` dedup on a\n  redelivered envelope. `pnpm vitest run` green across all 24\n  files / 122 tests; `cargo check --workspace` green; both specta\n  snapshot tests green.\n\n* stage 6: stage 6: Web UI — rewrite ui/codeless-ui/src/modules/jobs/ChatTab.tsx over the new RPCs; remove the existing transport-local message store (whatever shape it has today) and replace its in-memory list with a SWR-style cache keyed on job_id that rehydrates from list_job_messages on mount and appends from ChatMessageAppended on the SSE stream; pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green; the new vitest chat_tab_round_trips_a_post adds a fixture RpcClient and asserts the message input → post_job_message → ChatMessageAppended → render loop without touching the network\n\n* stage 7: Telegram adapter — chat forwarder + bind + delivery receipts\n\nAdds the chat-substrate inbound/outbound paths for Telegram from\nJOB-CHAT.md \"Transport adapters\":\n\n  - inbound long-poll now mirrors every text update in a bound\n    channel into chat_messages via post_job_message\n    (transport=telegram, external_id=<platform message_id>); the\n    partial unique index handles Telegram''s at-least-once redelivery\n    as a typed Conflict that the adapter drops silently.\n  - new inbound_chat module owns `/codeless bind <job_id>` and\n    writes the (transport, channel, thread) -> job_id row via\n    bind_chat_thread; the existing command dispatcher\n    (/status, /stop, /resume) keeps working in parallel.\n  - new chat module owns the outbound ChatMessageAppended\n    subscriber: skips Telegram-origin rows (echo suppression),\n    skips rows whose metadata.delivery.telegram is already set\n    (presence-based idempotency), fans every other row to every\n    Telegram binding for the Job, then records the delivery\n    receipt.\n\nSubstrate additions to support the forwarder:\n\n  - SqliteStore::update_chat_message_delivery — read-merge-write\n    inside a transaction; only metadata_json is touched, never\n    body or external_id (immutability bias).\n  - SqliteStore::list_chat_bindings_for_job — reverse lookup used\n    by every outbound forwarder.\n  - RpcServer::update_chat_message_delivery,\n    list_chat_bindings_for_job, get_chat_binding — minimal new\n    surface area, wired through http server + client + specta\n    wire snapshot.\n\nIntegration tests in crates/codeless-telegram/tests/chat_forwarder.rs\nstand up a real InProcessRpc + a wiremock-backed Telegram API and\nexercise the three behaviour bullets: web-origin → forwarded +\nreceipt written, telegram-origin → not echoed, prior receipt →\nnot re-forwarded.\n\n* stage 7: stage 7: Telegram adapter — codeless-telegram inbound writes via post_job_message with transport=''telegram'' and the platform message id as external_id; outbound subscribes to ChatMessageAppended and forwards every non-telegram-origin message via the Bot API, then writes the delivery receipt to chat_messages.metadata_json.delivery.telegram via a new update_chat_message_delivery store method (no UPDATE on body / external_id ever, per JOB-CHAT.md immutability bias); /codeless bind <job_id> writes to chat_bindings; idempotency is presence-based on metadata.delivery.telegram\n\n* stage 8: cold-load + echo-suppression — on /codeless bind, the Telegram adapter calls list_job_messages with the small N-tail and posts a single condensed ''joining mid-thread'' summary; the asymmetric echo-suppression rule moves into a shared helper in codeless-bot-core (origin-transport skip, non-origin forward + receipt), with bot_chat_e2e::origin_transport_skips_self_post and bot_chat_e2e::cross_transport_forwards_with_receipt as the load-bearing tests against an in-memory bus + a CannedTelegramApi\n\n* stage 8: stage 8: cold-load + echo-suppression — on /codeless bind, the Telegram adapter calls list_job_messages with the small N-tail and posts a single condensed ''joining mid-thread'' summary; the asymmetric echo-suppression rule moves into a shared helper in codeless-bot-core (origin-transport skip, non-origin forward + receipt), with bot_chat_e2e::origin_transport_skips_self_post and bot_chat_e2e::cross_transport_forwards_with_receipt as the load-bearing tests against an in-memory bus + a CannedTelegramApi\n\n* stage 9: M-C1-B — C1 end-to-end: a message typed in the web UI CHAT tab appears in a bound Telegram thread within one event tick (and vice versa); the punch list in JOB-CHAT.md §C1 punch-list is fully ticked off; JOB-CHAT.md status updated to record C1 as shipped; no supervisor yet\n\n* stage 9: supervisor module scaffold — codeless-runtime::supervisor\n\nJOB-CHAT.md (C2) stage 9. New `codeless-runtime::supervisor` module\n(not a crate; satisfies R1 by construction). `drive_job` spawns one\nsupervisor task per Run after the row transitions to `Running` and the\n`JobStarted` event is published; the task self-terminates on a Run\nterminal event (`JobCompleted` / `JobFailed` / `JobStopped`).\n`AwaitingReview` is non-terminal so the same conversation thread spans\na review gate; a fresh Run spawns a fresh supervisor.\n\nVoice contract: the supervisor''s only outbound channel is\n`post_job_message` with `transport=''supervisor''`. The module ships\nwith a `cargo test`-time grep that fails if any forbidden substring\n(stdlib print macros, loud tracing levels, direct event-bus publish)\nre-appears in the module source. The forbidden tokens are constructed\nfrom string fragments so the linter does not trip itself.\n\nTests:\n- `codeless_runtime::supervisor::lint::supervisor_module_source_has_no_forbidden_outbound_calls`\n- `codeless_runtime::supervisor::tests::{exits_on_job_completed, exits_on_job_failed, exits_on_job_stopped, stays_alive_through_non_terminal_events}`\n- `supervisor_e2e::supervisor_spawns_on_run_start_and_exits_on_run_terminal`\n- `supervisor_e2e::each_run_spawns_an_independent_supervisor`\n\n* stage 10: stage 9: supervisor module scaffold — codeless-runtime::supervisor (module, not crate; R1 by construction); spawned by drive_job when the Run enters Running; subscribed to ChatMessageAppended filtered to its job_id; cancelled on Run terminal status (Completed / Failed / Stopped / AwaitingReview is not terminal); a fresh Run spawns a fresh supervisor; supervisor_e2e::supervisor_spawns_on_run_start_and_exits_on_run_terminal green; the supervisor''s only outbound voice is post_job_message with transport=''supervisor'', which is enforced by grep + a clippy-style lint rule in the module''s mod.rs (no eprintln, no tracing::info that surfaces to user, no direct event publish)\n\n* stage 10: supervisor tool surface (read-only) — get_job_state, read_events, read_handover, read_template, read_stage_log, read_notes; plus the single write tool post_chat_message; each tool routes through existing RPCs / store reads so the supervisor never imports tokio::process or std::process (enforced by grep test in supervisor/mod.rs); supervisor_e2e::supervisor_answers_what_stage_is_it_on green using the mock runner + canned event timeline\n\nThe supervisor module now exposes a typed Tools struct alongside\nits lifecycle reactor. Tools owns Arc<EventBus> + Arc<SqliteStore>\nand an optional worktree_root for the filesystem-backed reads.\n\nTool surface\n  - get_job_state — store.get_job + list_stages_for_job, projects a\n    JobStateView { status, started_at, current_stage, stage_count }\n    where current_stage is the highest-ordinal Running row falling\n    back to the highest-ordinal row overall.\n  - read_events — bus.fetch_recent_for_job, a new pub method on\n    EventBus that pulls the events table tail for one job in cursor-\n    ascending order, clamped to READ_EVENTS_LIMIT_MAX = 500.\n  - read_handover — tokio::fs::read_to_string on the conventional\n    runs/<job_id>/<stage_id>/handover.md path via the existing\n    handover::handover_path helper.\n  - read_template — job row''s template_yaml column. None for prompt-\n    only jobs.\n  - read_stage_log — runs/<job_id>/<stage_id>/log.md.\n  - read_notes — collects runs/<job_id>/notes/*.md sorted by filename.\n  - post_chat_message — the supervisor''s only voice. Inserts a row\n    with transport=Supervisor, role=Assistant, author=\"supervisor\",\n    then publishes Event::ChatMessageAppended. Mirrors the contract\n    of rpc::job_chat::post_job_message.\n\nReactor\n  spawn_supervisor_with_tools(bus, store, job_id) wires the C2\n  question/answer loop: on a non-supervisor ChatMessageAppended whose\n  body contains \"what stage\" / \"which stage\", call get_job_state and\n  reply via post_chat_message. The original spawn_supervisor entry\n  point keeps its bus-only signature for the existing driver wiring\n  and lifecycle tests.\n\nLint\n  The grep in supervisor::lint now also rejects literal mentions of\n  the stdlib process module and tokio''s process module — keeping R1\n  of CLAUDE.md and Hard rule 2 of JOB-CHAT.md grep-enforced. Doc-\n  comments avoid the forbidden literals via word-wrap so the lint\n  source itself stays clean.\n\nTests\n  - supervisor::tools::tests pin get_job_state''s \"pick Running stage\"\n    rule and post_chat_message''s insert + publish shape.\n  - supervisor_e2e::supervisor_answers_what_stage_is_it_on stands up\n    a job, inserts a Stage row at ordinal 10, posts a \"what stage is\n    it on?\" message via the Web transport, then asserts a Supervisor\n    ChatMessageAppended envelope arrives within 2s whose body cites\n    \"stage 10\". The canned event timeline is the inserted Stage row +\n    the Web-origin chat post; no live mock runner is needed because\n    the tool surface reads from SQLite, not from the live event tail.\n\ncargo test -p codeless-runtime --lib supervisor green (7/7).\ncargo test -p codeless-runtime --test supervisor_e2e green (3/3).\ncargo clippy -p codeless-runtime --all-targets -- -D warnings clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 11: stage 10: supervisor tool surface (read-only) — get_job_state, read_events, read_handover, read_template, read_stage_log, read_notes; plus the single write tool post_chat_message; each tool routes through existing RPCs / store reads so the supervisor never imports tokio::process or std::process (enforced by grep test in supervisor/mod.rs); supervisor_e2e::supervisor_answers_what_stage_is_it_on green using the mock runner + canned event timeline\n\n* stage 11: supervisor model + prompt — supervisor/prompt.rs holds the system prompt + per-tool descriptions as reviewer-readable text (kept under 4 KB so a typical Haiku reply stays under the 2c-per-turn budget) and the deterministic format_terminal_summary helper; supervisor/claude.rs wires a one-shot ClaudeRunnerAdapter behind the new supervisor-claude cargo feature, never naming std::process or tokio::process so R1 + the lint test still hold; on Run terminal envelope the supervisor''s run_with_tools loop reads the stage list (including failure_detail) via tools.store_arc() and posts a one-paragraph summary citing each stage name before exiting; driver.rs now spawns the tool-equipped supervisor so the summary fires in production; supervisor_e2e::supervisor_posts_terminal_summary_on_run_failed green\n\n* stage 12: stage 11: supervisor model + prompt — wire the supervisor''s Claude session via the existing claude runner under a host-only feature flag; system prompt + tool descriptions live in supervisor/prompt.rs so they are reviewable as text; on Run terminal status the supervisor posts a one-paragraph summary referencing the stage names and visible failure_detail then exits; the prompt is short enough that a typical reply costs <2c at current pricing\n\n* stage 13: M-C2 — C2 end-to-end: with a Run in Running state, typing what-stage-is-it-on in the web CHAT tab gets a real supervisor reply within a few seconds that references the current stage name and the most recent ai-token; supervisor_e2e is green; JOB-CHAT.md status updated to record C2 as shipped\n\n* stage 12: action tools — stop_job and add_job_note in supervisor/tools/actions.rs; both route through the existing SqliteStore + EventBus surfaces so the resulting JobStopped envelope is byte-identical to the UI [stop] button''s and the ChatMessageAppended row carries the same shape every transport renders; the events row has no actor column (the table predates the concept), so the per-action provenance lives on the chat row''s transport=''supervisor''; ad-hoc preview window is a 5-second tokio::time::sleep with a cancellation check on a fresh ChatMessageAppended from a user role containing /^wait\\b/i, with the post-action summary / cancellation row''s metadata.resolves pointing back at the preview row id; supervisor_e2e::ad_hoc_stop_aborts_on_user_wait and supervisor_e2e::ad_hoc_stop_fires_after_window green\n\n* stage 14: stage 12: action tools — stop_job and add_job_note in supervisor/tools/actions.rs; both route through existing RPCs and emit the same JobCancelled / JobNoteAdded events the UI buttons produce, with the events row''s actor=''supervisor'' so the audit trail tells you which surface triggered; ad-hoc preview window is a 5-second tokio::time::sleep with a cancellation check on a fresh ChatMessageAppended from a user role containing /^wait\\b/i; supervisor_e2e::ad_hoc_stop_aborts_on_user_wait and supervisor_e2e::ad_hoc_stop_fires_after_window green\n\n* stage 13: supervisor_goals migration + store\n\nAdd migrations/0026_supervisor_goals.sql with the closed kind set\n(deadline-stop / threshold-stop / event-notify), typed condition /\naction payloads validated at write time, and the armed → fired |\ncancelled | superseded status walk JOB-CHAT.md §C3 specifies. The\npartial index on (run_id, created_at) WHERE status=''armed'' is what\nkeeps supervisor-boot rehydration cheap as terminal rows accumulate\nacross a Run''s lifetime.\n\nstore::supervisor_goals exposes insert_goal / list_armed_for_run /\nmark_fired / mark_cancelled / mark_superseded. Validation is in Rust\n(SQLite has no native enum) and runs through SupervisorGoalKind /\nGoalCondition / GoalAction; mismatched kind/condition triples cannot\nland on disk, and a row written with the wrong serialised shape\nsurfaces a typed decode error rather than a silent no-op. The\nmark_* helpers are idempotent — second-call against a terminal row\nreturns MarkOutcome::NoChange — so a deadline arm racing with a\nuser cancel can lose cleanly.\n\npause_after_stage rides in GoalAction; round-trips through insert /\nlist, surfaces ExecutionState::NoOpFailed so the supervisor reactor\ncan short-circuit without invoking a runner-side pause. JOB-WORKFLOW\n(A.5) is the affordance that flips this to Ready.\n\n* stage 15: stage 13: supervisor_goals migration + store — codeless-runtime/migrations/0026_supervisor_goals.sql with the columns from JOB-CHAT.md §C3 (id, run_id, kind, condition_json, action_json, authorised_by, status, created_at, fired_at); codeless-runtime::store::supervisor_goals with insert_goal / list_armed_for_run / mark_fired / mark_cancelled / mark_superseded; goal_kinds in v0.1 = deadline-stop, threshold-stop, event-notify; condition_json and action_json validated against a typed enum at write time; pause_after_stage is parsed but produces a structured Failed (no-op until JOB-WORKFLOW (A.5))\n\n* stage 14: pre-armed action loop — supervisor watches its goals via a select! loop combining the event bus, a deadline timer, and a tokio::time::sleep_until per armed goal; on fire, the action invokes immediately (no preview, per Hard rule 4 of JOB-CHAT.md) and the supervisor posts the ''I just did X because Y'' summary as a chat row referencing the authorising chat_messages.id; supervisor_e2e::deadline_stop_fires_at_t_plus_one_hour green using a tokio time-driver pause + advance\n\nWires the pre-armed firing path inside codeless-runtime/src/supervisor/mod.rs: on boot the supervisor rehydrates armed supervisor_goals rows via list_armed_for_run and pushes one Pin<Box<dyn Future>> per deadline-stop goal onto a FuturesUnordered (threshold-stop / event-notify variants stay out of scope until their respective metric / event sources land). The reactor''s select! loop now races the existing event-bus stream against the timer set; the goals branch carries an `if !timers.is_empty()` guard so an empty FuturesUnordered does not hot-spin the select.\n\nfire_goal owns the race against a concurrent user cancellation: it calls Tools::mark_goal_fired first (the SqliteStore''s UPDATE … WHERE status=''armed'' serialises the writers) and only invokes the action when the transition lands. The Tools surface gains fire_pre_armed_goal and mark_goal_fired so the reactor''s seam to persistence stays single-source — the supervisor module never touches the store module directly. The post-action chat row carries metadata.replies_to = authorised_by and metadata.goal_id = goal.id so the audit trail JOB-CHAT.md Hard rule 4 promises is a foreign-key edge readable through the normal list_chat_messages surface.\n\ndeadline arming: arm_goal_timer stores absolute wall-clock deadlines (deadline_ms - now_ms() at boot) and the per-arm sleep is a tokio::time::sleep over that delta, so the production path waits on real wall time and the test path drives mock tokio time. A past deadline yields a zero-duration sleep so a restart that lands after the original deadline still catches the missed fire on the first select tick.\n\nsupervisor_e2e::deadline_stop_fires_at_t_plus_one_hour pauses tokio time *after* spawning the supervisor (so subscribe_since and list_armed_for_run go through sqlx without the paused timer-driver starving the pool), advances 3_600_001ms past the deadline, then resumes time so the fire flow''s sqlx hops (mark_fired / update_job / insert_chat_message) and broadcast wake-ups complete. The test asserts: a) the post-fire summary references the authorising MessageId and the goal id in metadata, b) no System-role preview row was emitted (pre-armed actions skip the preview window, per Hard rule 4''s second regime), c) the job row transitions to Stopped and the goal leaves `armed`. tokio dev-deps gain the test-util feature for time::pause + time::advance.\n\n* stage 16: stage 14: pre-armed action loop — supervisor watches its goals via a select! loop combining the event bus, a deadline timer, and a tokio::time::sleep_until per armed goal; on fire, the action invokes immediately (no preview, per Hard rule 4 of JOB-CHAT.md) and the supervisor posts the ''I just did X because Y'' summary as a chat row referencing the authorising chat_messages.id; supervisor_e2e::deadline_stop_fires_at_t_plus_one_hour green using a tokio time-driver pause + advance\n\n* stage 15: goal rehydration on supervisor boot — `run_with_tools` scans `armed` rows for its `run_id` on every spawn (including a fresh process after a server bounce), re-arms a `tokio::time::sleep` per deadline-stop row, and walks rows whose condition can no longer trip to `superseded` with a `transport=''supervisor''` chat note quoting the reason; threshold-stop / event-notify rows stay armed pending their signal-source wiring; tests `supervisor_e2e::supervisor_rehydrates_deadline_after_restart` and `supervisor_e2e::supervisor_supersedes_armed_goals_when_run_is_terminal_at_boot` green — the rehydrate test drops the first supervisor mid-Run with `abort()`, spawns a fresh one, then `tokio::time::pause`/`advance`s past the deadline to assert the goal still fires with the same authorising-chat-id metadata edge\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 17: stage 15: goal rehydration on supervisor boot — a supervisor that starts (or restarts after a server bounce mid-Run) scans armed rows for its run_id, re-arms timers / event watchers, marks rows whose condition no longer makes sense as Superseded with the reason; supervisor_e2e::supervisor_rehydrates_deadline_after_restart green (drop the supervisor mid-run, spawn a fresh one, advance time, assert the goal still fires)\n\n* stage 16: Slack adapter parity — new codeless-slack chat.rs forwarder mirrors codeless-telegram::chat one-for-one against the Slack Web API; ChatTransport::Slack rows in chat_bindings drive fan-out, ChatPoster::post returns the parent ts that lands as metadata_json.delivery.slack via update_chat_message_delivery; the shared echo-suppression decision lives in codeless_bot_core::chat_forward::classify so adding the second transport is a one-line caller change rather than a re-derived rule; SlackBot::spawn_with always wires a ChatForwarder (drained in shutdown alongside the outbound failure-card publisher); slack_chat_e2e mirrors bot_chat_e2e against a CannedSlackApi wiremock with cross_transport_forwards_with_receipt and origin_transport_skips_self_post green\n\n* stage 18: stage 16: Slack adapter parity — new codeless-slack crate following the codeless-telegram shape; same chat_bindings.transport=''slack'' rows; the shared echo-suppression + outbound-receipt helper in codeless-bot-core is the only thing that needs to know two transports exist; slack_chat_e2e mirrors bot_chat_e2e against a CannedSlackApi\n\n* stage 17: documentation + handover — JOB-CHAT.md Status rows for C1, C2, C3 record the per-Job chat substrate, supervisor module, and pre-armed-goals work landed on codeless/job-chat with the load-bearing test names; resolved-OQ updates already inline since stage 1; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' lines for the three landed surfaces (unified chat table, read-only supervisor, action tools + supervisor_goals); migration-list test grew supervisor_goals so the appendix-A invariant matches the actual schema; cargo test --workspace, cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo fmt --check, pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint, and pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test all green\n\nDeferred (not in this job''s scope): mcp_forward parity from PLUGIN-MCP is unrelated and stays in its own plugin track; mobile-shell wiring of the new chat surfaces is Phase 6, since codeless-tauri-mobile only sees codeless-types and codeless-client and the substrate already meets that boundary; multi-user trust beyond the chat_bindings.bound_by operator (per-user OIDC + per-action ACLs) is Phase 7 OIDC per OQ-CHAT-3.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 20: stage 17: documentation + handover — JOB-CHAT.md Status rows for C1, C2, C3; resolved-OQ updates inline; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' lines for the three landed surfaces; final cargo test --workspace + cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings + cargo fmt --check + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green; one paragraph in the job handover summarising what''s deferred (mcp_forward parity from PLUGIN-MCP is unrelated; mobile-shell wiring of chat surfaces is Phase 6; multi-user trust is Phase 7 OIDC)\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqC5iCCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAASSIQAAmu6IP+NZD4UQ26HFOt84PA\nThYc19M+bnYIDyzh/Oc3aNfm1D1/l6QP9bqqzojOTN5EcYffp0hX5MEPRJXymlVj\nSEZd4dqf9JpeDC8kIapMEv/sj8ioiel2v0vHNRTofUbTOfiQljpa+gvxoXu52kK2\nqKFbBqc7YjyPWcTCOhFAoaqOOeTf2EZk8mrme2QEPwUB3dUVDf03AZN2q2qYBOPa\n6ktvdZxC/566Ytr/IarpLX48gbozHX+YlZtJbwE3irFfXQgyf10GCouejbZiUHJq\n8L1mNv/0xqPtehBaA7Pkg61FDoNHP2+ejc1D+jvwMrqtXLo4bCDBrgnk6dVtCqAQ\n2K3qVZKA1ff44dV+i5ho4LuyHFq7FdZlgF+5Reqn0Rfn872HaU6+0FPKt9HWGpAK\ny60YQUfXfSPgqT78TjruSnA9zrupFnFbYgsv+8vZEUe05dOvreXsBjLVZUEwSWay\n0l3lG2J9sW7ND3pDePwsGSjTSi13hUWnowE/Cjsb8gfyLtRRLfJU2q5sKJx9INl9\nAh3VIJzKIKCs5wFKJfG3I4Qei9W0LFA+5SdP3NFdV/BjEjifgXMeFV+EE4m3v6ff\nMg3n2ZQ7BYpNI24o4DHgFR5xtBPfHJRf8RB3FQNp5r3E3m1P1GpqfeN5GbYi4pmm\nyx3gEHNOCBOCjbd+3Bqw\n=76PU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-05-18T22:53:54Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/codeless/commit/fa6a2fb877d01865a1030eeb5433fc130a8aaaa7","node_id":"C_kwDOSa1B6doAKGZhNmEyZmI4NzdkMDE4NjVhMTAzMGVlYjU0MzNmYzEzMGE4YWFhYTc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev/codeless/commit/6d8ab52604fda32d2c38d5a4e12447a679cfaaf1","sha":"6d8ab52604fda32d2c38d5a4e12447a679cfaaf1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/6d8ab52604fda32d2c38d5a4e12447a679cfaaf1"}],"sha":"fa6a2fb877d01865a1030eeb5433fc130a8aaaa7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/fa6a2fb877d01865a1030eeb5433fc130a8aaaa7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18637436?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NubeDev","id":18637436,"login":"NubeDev","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NjM3NDM2","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NubeDev","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/commits/6d8ab52604fda32d2c38d5a4e12447a679cfaaf1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-05-18T22:48:50Z","email":"aidan@enviroservices.com.au","name":"Aidan P"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-05-18T22:48:50Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"plugin-substrate: WASM runtime, MF plugin UI (R6), process seam, MCP contributions (#33)\n\n* stage 1: resolve open questions (OQ-WASM-1, -2, -4, -5; OQ-MCP-1; OQ-UI-1; final v0.1 slot vocab) under SCOPE.md §Open questions and propagate the resolutions into the four plugin docs; no implementation\n\n* stage 0: stage 1: resolve open questions (OQ-WASM-1, -2, -4, -5; OQ-MCP-1; OQ-UI-1; final v0.1 slot vocab) under SCOPE.md §Open questions and propagate the resolutions into the four plugin docs; no implementation\n\n* stage 2: scaffold codeless-plugin-sdk (mobile-safe) with the rubix-lifted compile_error! mutual-exclusion guard for native/wasm/process features, the ToolBehavior trait, the #[derive(Tool)] macro (schemars-driven), and the register! macro stub; the existing notes plugin keeps compiling unchanged via a thin shim\n\nNew crates:\n\n- crates/codeless-plugin-sdk: mobile-safe authoring SDK. Pulls in\n  schemars, async-trait, serde, thiserror; no host deps. Carries\n  ToolMeta (compile-time id/tier/description), ToolBehavior\n  (Args/Output/call -- the trait every plugin tool implements),\n  ToolCtx (opaque seam the per-flavour adapter fills in later),\n  ToolError (variants line up 1:1 with tool-error.code in\n  tool.wit), Tier, Manifest::for_behavior::<T>() (schemars-driven\n  input/output schemas), and the register! macro stub.\n\n- crates/codeless-plugin-sdk-macros: proc-macro crate exposing\n  #[derive(Tool)] -- reads #[tool(id, tier, description)] and\n  emits the ToolMeta impl. Pattern lifted from rubix\n  extensions-sdk-macros (NodeKind); body new since codeless''s\n  manifest is schemars-derived at runtime, not embedded from YAML.\n\nThe compile_error! mutual-exclusion guard for builtin/wasm/process\nis copied from rubix-workspace/extension-sdk/extensions-sdk/src/\nlib.rs -- the failure mode is the same and a runtime-rebuilt guard\nwould diverge.\n\nregister! is intentionally a stub today. It type-checks that the\nargument implements ToolBehavior, then expands to nothing. The\nper-flavour expansion (inventory entry for builtin, wit-bindgen\nexports for wasm) lands in later stages of the\nplugin-substrate-runtimes job; plugin source written against the\nSDK today survives that with no diff.\n\nThe notes plugin keeps compiling unchanged: it still implements\ncodeless_tools::Tool directly. The SDK is parallel infrastructure\nfor the upcoming runtime-flavour work; a future stage migrates\nnotes onto ToolBehavior via the builtin-flavour host shim.\n\nPinned by crates/codeless-plugin-sdk/tests/derive_smoke.rs: derive\nemits the expected constants, Manifest::for_behavior round-trips\nschemars schemas through the manifest, ToolBehavior::call\ndispatches, and register!() compiles against a real\nToolBehavior impl.\n\nRefs: DOCS/plugins/PLUGIN-SUBSTRATE.md \"Reuse from rubix\",\nDOCS/plugins/PLUGIN-WASM.md \"The authoring SDK\".\n\n* stage 3: scaffold codeless-tool-wit with tool.wit (codeless tool ABI per PLUGIN-WASM.md), commit the wit-bindgen-generated bindings in-tree (OQ-WASM-2 resolution), and a doc-only Rust smoke test that loads the WIT and round-trips a manifest\n\n* stage 2: stage 3: scaffold codeless-tool-wit with tool.wit (codeless tool ABI per PLUGIN-WASM.md), commit the wit-bindgen-generated bindings in-tree (OQ-WASM-2 resolution), and a doc-only Rust smoke test that loads the WIT and round-trips a manifest\n\n* stage 4: scaffold codeless-plugin-host-wasm (host-only) — Wasmtime engine, WASI-p2 component-model linker, per-call instantiation, HostPolicy fuel/memory/wall-clock caps; expose a WasmAdapter implementing the runtime-adapter trait introduced in this stage in codeless-tools\n\n- Add `codeless-tools::runtime_adapter` (mobile-safe trait, table,\n  request/outcome/manifest types, collisions). Per OQ-WASM-1 the\n  table lives in `codeless-tools`; the trait carries no host-only\n  types (pure data + `async_trait`) so the iOS/Android cargo-check\n  matrix cannot leak `wasmtime` through this seam.\n- New host-only crate `codeless-plugin-host-wasm` (wasmtime 23 +\n  wasmtime-wasi 23, async + consume_fuel):\n  - `WasmRuntime` -- process-wide engine + linker prototype, WASI\n    default-deny on construction.\n  - `WasmPlugin` -- per-call instantiation; fuel/memory/deadline\n    caps from `HostPolicy` (defaults: 100M / 64 MiB / 10 s per\n    PLUGIN-WASM.md § Limits); host-side `wasmtime::component::\n    bindgen!` against `codeless-tool-wit/wit/tool.wit` for the\n    typed `describe`/`call` accessors.\n  - `WasmAdapter` -- `RuntimeAdapter` impl that forwards\n    `manifests()` and `call()` into `WasmPlugin`; one adapter per\n    plugin, N table entries pointing at the shared `Arc`.\n  - `HostPolicy::with_override` enforces \"overrides may only lower\"\n    per OQ-WASM-5 so a config-parse can fail-fast on a manifest\n    that tries to enlarge the sandbox.\n- Wasmtime trap classification (`OutOfFuel` -> `fuel`,\n  `MemoryOutOfBounds` -> `memory`, `tokio::time::timeout` ->\n  `deadline`) maps to `tool-error.code = \"limit-exceeded\"` at the\n  adapter boundary so stage 7''s end-to-end fuel test has a single\n  vocabulary to assert against.\n- Tests: runtime_adapter unit tests (register, collisions, error\n  builder); host-wasm unit tests (invalid component bytes, trap\n  classifier); host-wasm smoke test (runtime constructs, defaults\n  match doc, `RuntimeAdapter` is object-safe). End-to-end load of\n  a `.wasm` is stage 5''s owner.\n- `cargo test --workspace`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets\n  -- -D warnings`, and `cargo fmt --check` all green; codeless-\n  client''s dep tree carries no wasmtime or codeless-plugin-host-\n  wasm.\n\n* stage 3: stage 4: scaffold codeless-plugin-host-wasm (host-only) — Wasmtime engine, WASI-p2 component-model linker, per-call instantiation, HostPolicy fuel/memory/wall-clock caps; expose a WasmAdapter implementing the runtime-adapter trait introduced in this stage in codeless-tools\n\n* stage 4: M-WASM-A — codeless-plugin-sdk + codeless-tool-wit + codeless-plugin-host-wasm crates compile, the runtime-adapter table is registered in codeless-tools, no host-only types leaked into mobile-safe builds (verify via the iOS/Android cargo check matrix); no plugin loads anything yet\n\n* stage 5: port codeless-plugin-notes onto codeless-plugin-sdk -- same source, two flavours (builtin via cargo build, WASM via cargo build --target wasm32-wasip2); plugin_substrate_e2e::notes_plugin_loads_and_seeds_persona_addressable_by_thread parameterised over both flavours, both green\n\n- Rewrite NotesAppend on top of `codeless_plugin_sdk::ToolBehavior` with\n  typed `NotesAppendArgs` / `NotesAppendOutput`; the PS7 attachment marker\n  reaches `/properties/attachment/$ref` via a schemars `schema_with` hook.\n- Builtin bridge `BuiltinBridge<T: ToolBehavior>` inside the notes crate\n  adapts ToolBehavior -> `codeless_tools::Tool`, preserving the existing\n  `register` entry point. Lives in the plugin so the SDK -> host dep\n  direction stays one-way.\n- WASM flavour: cdylib + mutually-exclusive `builtin` / `wasm` features.\n  `#[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")] wasm_guest` invokes\n  `wit_bindgen::generate!`, impls `Guest::describe` / `Guest::call`\n  (pollster::block_on for the sync<->async bridge), `export!`s the\n  component. Crate-level `unsafe_code = \"deny\"` exception mirrors\n  codeless-tool-wit.\n- Bump `wasmtime` 23 -> 30 in codeless-plugin-host-wasm: LLVM 19 (rustc\n  >= 1.85) emits overlong LEB128 memory-index-0 encodings wasmparser\n  0.212 rejects; wasmtime 27+ accepts them. WasiView::table() migrated\n  to the new IoView supertrait in wasmtime-wasi 30.\n- Parameterise the substrate e2e test:\n  `notes_plugin_loads_and_seeds_persona_addressable_by_thread_builtin`\n  reads notes.append off the host ToolRegistry; the `_wasm` row builds\n  the same source via `cargo build --target wasm32-unknown-unknown\n  --no-default-features --features wasm`, composes into a WASI-p2\n  component with wit-component 0.212, and reads the manifest off a\n  WasmPlugin::load''ed component''s describe() export. Both rows pin the\n  PS7 attachment marker.\n\nNotable deviation: the e2e test builds via wasm32-unknown-unknown +\nwit-component, not wasm32-wasip2. rustc''s bundled WASI preview1->2\nadapter emits a component-model encoding wasmtime 30 still rejects\n(adapter-internal offset error); the notes plugin world has no WASI\nimports anyway, so wasm32-unknown-unknown + manual componentise is the\nclean path. PLUGIN-WASM.md doc reconciliation deferred to stage 15.\n\n* stage 5: stage 5: port codeless-plugin-notes onto codeless-plugin-sdk — same source, two flavours (builtin via cargo build, WASM via cargo build --target wasm32-wasip2); plugin_substrate_e2e::notes_plugin_loads_and_seeds_persona_addressable_by_thread parameterised over both flavours, both green\n\n* stage 6: implement WASM capability sandbox -- default-deny capability set; [runtimes.capabilities] manifest parsing; attachments R/W via the host-implemented codeless:attachments/store WIT interface; plugin_wasm_e2e::wasm_plugin_cannot_open_host_file + plugin_wasm_e2e::wasm_plugin_attachment_round_trip green\n\nPer PLUGIN-WASM.md § Capability sandbox the per-plugin linker is now\nconstructed from the manifest''s [runtimes.capabilities] grants; an\nempty set keeps every host-implemented WIT interface unlinked, so a\ncomponent importing one fails at instantiation rather than at a\ncall-time denial. New WIT packages codeless:attachments@0.1.0 +\ncodeless:fs@0.1.0 (under wit/deps/) back two companion worlds in\ntool.wit (plugin-with-attachments, plugin-with-fs) and the host\ncrate ships the AttachmentStore trait + InMemoryAttachmentStore for\nthe e2e round-trip. The plugin.toml parser gains [[runtimes]] entries\nwith strict-validated kinds (builtin / wasm / process) and a typed\ncapabilities sub-block that rejects fuel/memory/deadline at parse\ntime (OQ-WASM-5).\n\n* stage 8: implement fuel + memory + wall-clock caps; plugin_wasm_e2e::wasm_plugin_respects_fuel_cap green within the 200ms deadline; manifest accepts but the plugin cannot self-raise limits\n\nAdds the `fuel_loop` test fixture (default `plugin` world, infinite\nblack_box-guarded arithmetic loop) and the\n`wasm_plugin_respects_fuel_cap` e2e test. The test loads the\nfixture under HostPolicy { fuel = 100_000, deadline = 200 ms } and\nasserts the call surfaces ToolCallOutcome::Err with code\n`limit-exceeded` and a message naming `fuel`, completing inside\nthe 200 ms budget. The fuel/memory/deadline plumbing was already\nwired through `WasmPlugin::call` in stage 4; this stage closes the\nloop with the assertion that proves it. The manifest-side rule\n(`[runtimes.capabilities] fuel = ...` is rejected) is already\npinned by `manifest::tests::rejects_unknown_capability_field`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 7: stage 7: implement fuel + memory + wall-clock caps; plugin_wasm_e2e::wasm_plugin_respects_fuel_cap green within the 200ms deadline; manifest accepts but the plugin cannot self-raise limits\n\n* stage 8: fork rubix extension-ui-sdk into ui/codeless-ui/packages/plugin-ui-sdk/\n\nFork rubix-workspace/extension-ui-sdk@a7fecef into a new in-tree\npackage and rename the inbound imports to the codeless namespace\n(@rubix/agent-client -> @codeless/rpc, @rubix/ui-core -> @codeless/ui-core,\n@rubix/ui-kit -> @codeless/ui-kit). Every ported file carries a\n// codeless-ported-from: <rubix-path>@<sha> header so the rename is\nauditable. Rubix git history is dropped — only the source content is\nported, never .git.\n\nSet up pnpm workspaces in ui/codeless-ui/ (packages/* + the root app)\nso the new SDK package is wired into the host''s install graph. Add a\nminimal @codeless/ui-core package containing the graph-history bits\n(CommandScope, useCommandStack, useCommandStackStore, useGraphHistory,\ncreateGraphHistoryStore) ported from rubix-ui-core@59b23f4 so the\neditable-collection test bed has a working ui-core to import from.\nStage 9 fills the rest of the SDK API surface; the remaining ported\nhooks/components (useAction, useSlotWriter, BlockShell, ...) ship\nwith their codeless-ported-from headers but are excluded from\ntsconfig until @codeless/rpc and @codeless/ui-kit land.\n\nAcceptance:\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui/packages/plugin-ui-sdk test green\n  (21/21 in editable-collection.test.tsx under codeless''s vitest +\n  jsdom config — same test body as the rubix original)\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui/packages/plugin-ui-sdk typecheck clean\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui/packages/ui-core typecheck clean\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui typecheck clean (host unaffected)\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green (host 118/118)\n\n* stage 9: stage 8: fork rubix extension-ui-sdk into ui/codeless-ui/packages/plugin-ui-sdk/, rename @rubix/agent-client→@codeless/rpc and @rubix/ui-core→@codeless/ui-core, drop rubix git history; every ported file carries // codeless-ported-from: <rubix-path>@<sha>; package builds and the rubix-derived editable-collection.test.tsx passes under codeless''s vitest config\n\n* stage 9: implement @codeless/plugin-ui-sdk MF surface\n\nWire the host-facing MF surface plugin authors need to ship a remote\ninto the codeless UI shell:\n\n- slots.ts: v0.1 slot vocabulary (assistant-panel, tool-result,\n  persona-picker, settings-page, composer-attachment-action) with\n  parameter parsing + ownership shape per\n  DOCS/plugins/PLUGIN-UI-FEDERATION.md.\n- mf.ts: MfRuntime install seam so the host wires\n  @module-federation/enhanced once at boot; pluginManifestUrl helper\n  keeps the /plugins/<id>/ui/mf-manifest.json layout single-sourced.\n- registration.ts: process-global slot-contribution registry with\n  subscribe/notify so a runtime enable/disable cycle re-renders\n  mounted PluginSlot instances.\n- components/PluginSlot.tsx: the single mount component the host\n  renders at every slot site; lazy-loads each contributor through\n  the installed MfRuntime, isolates failures per-contributor in an\n  error boundary, and forwards the parameterised slot arg as\n  slotArg.\n- rsbuild-shared.ts: authoritative shared-singleton pin map (React\n  19, react-dom 19, zustand 5, @tanstack/react-query 5, the three\n  @codeless/* internal packages); the source of truth for both the\n  host''s and every plugin''s rsbuild.config.ts shared field.\n- eslint-config.ts: ESLint flat-config enforcing R6 — no\n  @tauri-apps/*, no own React/zustand/tanstack-query, no fetch/XHR\n  in plugin sources; rsbuild.config.* carved out.\n\nEach module ships with tests pinning the contract host wiring and\nPLUGIN-UI-FEDERATION.md depend on (56 tests; package.json /\ntsconfig / vitest.config updated). Host UI typecheck stays green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 10: stage 9: implement @codeless/plugin-ui-sdk: mf.ts (slot vocabulary as resolved in stage 1), registration.ts, PluginSlot React component, rsbuild-shared.ts (singleton version pin source of truth), eslint-config.ts enforcing R6\n\n* stage 10: host shell wiring in ui/codeless-ui/ — rpc.plugins.list() at boot, MF host registration of each enabled plugin remote, <PluginSlot id=…/> mounted at the resolved slot sites; the host renders a fallback when a slot has no contributors\n\n* stage 11: stage 10: host shell wiring in ui/codeless-ui/ — rpc.plugins.list() at boot, MF host registration of each enabled plugin remote, <PluginSlot id=…/> mounted at the resolved slot sites; the host renders a fallback when a slot has no contributors\n\n* stage 11: server REST surface — GET /plugins reflecting the registry, GET /plugins/<id>/ui/* via ServeDir rooted at the plugin''s ui/ subdir; bearer-token auth on the listing, ServeDir matching the rest of the UI bundle''s posture\n\n* stage 12: stage 11: server REST surface — GET /plugins reflecting the registry, GET /plugins/<id>/ui/* via ServeDir rooted at the plugin''s ui/ subdir; bearer-token auth on the listing, ServeDir matching the rest of the UI bundle''s posture\n\n* stage 12: notes plugin ui/ subtree — minimal AssistantPanel remote (list recent notes via rpc.tools.call(notes.list_recent, …)) built with the plugin''s rsbuild config that imports @codeless/plugin-ui-sdk/rsbuild-shared; plugin_ui_e2e::host_loads_plugin_remote_and_mounts_assistant_panel + plugin_ui_e2e::mismatched_react_fails_loudly + plugin_ui_e2e::r6_eslint_rejects_forbidden_imports green\n\n* stage 13: stage 12: notes plugin ui/ subtree — minimal AssistantPanel remote (list recent notes via rpc.tools.call(notes.list_recent, …)) built with the plugin''s rsbuild config that imports @codeless/plugin-ui-sdk/rsbuild-shared; plugin_ui_e2e::host_loads_plugin_remote_and_mounts_assistant_panel + plugin_ui_e2e::mismatched_react_fails_loudly + plugin_ui_e2e::r6_eslint_rejects_forbidden_imports green\n\n* stage 13: codeless-server manifest parser - [[runtimes]] kind in {builtin, wasm, process} with kind-specific blocks; [runtimes.capabilities]; [runtimes.policy]; two-phase scan lifted from rubix extensions-host so a partial failure cannot half-populate registries; plugin_substrate_e2e::process_runtime_declared_today_loads_failed_with_structured_reason green\n\nManifest parser:\n- PluginRuntime gains kind-specific fields: `binary` (process) joins\n  `crate` (builtin) and `artefact` (wasm); validate_runtime_kind_block\n  enforces required-vs-not-applicable per kind so a `binary` on a\n  builtin entry fails at parse rather than being silently ignored.\n- New `[runtimes.policy]` sub-block (`PluginRuntimePolicy`):\n  socket_ready_timeout, health_interval, failure_threshold,\n  failure_window, failed_cooldown. Duration strings (\"5s\", \"100ms\",\n  \"2m\") parsed at manifest-load via a narrow `parse_duration`\n  helper. `failed_cooldown` is a TOML sum-type via `#[serde(untagged)]`\n  accepting literal `false` (sticky-Failed default) or a duration\n  string (headless retry); literal `true` rejected as meaningless.\n  Wasm and builtin entries reject any policy fields\n  (RuntimePolicyNotApplicable).\n- ResolvedPluginRuntimePolicy exposes typed Durations the future\n  process supervisor will consume; the raw manifest strings remain\n  on PluginRuntimePolicy so the serde round-trip preserves operator-\n  typed shape.\n\nTwo-phase substrate scanner (crates/codeless-tools/src/plugin/\nsubstrate.rs):\n- scan_plugins_dir(dir, &RegistrationTable) -> ScanResult walks a\n  parent dir, finds plugins via plugin.toml. Phase 1 vets each\n  manifest in isolation; phase 2 commits the vetted plugins into a\n  single PluginRegistry. A failure in either phase scopes to its\n  plugin without leaking partial state into the others, matching\n  rubix extensions-host''s two-phase shape.\n- PluginLoadOutcome::{Loaded, Failed, Unparseable} carries enough\n  state on every branch for `codeless plugin info` to render a\n  declared-but-unsupported plugin''s manifest, not just successes.\n- PluginFailureReason::ProcessRuntimeNotSupported is the\n  PLUGIN-PROCESS.md § Reserve-the-seam outcome with the stable\n  code string \"process-runtime-not-supported\"; UnknownBuiltin and\n  NoLoadableRuntime cover the other today-recoverable cases.\n- resolve_active_runtime picks builtin > wasm > process when a\n  plugin ships multiple [[runtimes]] entries.\n\nTests:\n- 12 new manifest unit tests covering required-vs-not-applicable\n  per kind, duration parsing edge cases, the failed_cooldown\n  literal-vs-duration sum-type, and policy applicability per kind.\n- 5 new substrate unit tests covering scan_plugins_dir end-to-end,\n  process-only failure, mixed-vet, unparseable manifests, and\n  unknown-builtin failure.\n- plugin_substrate_e2e::process_runtime_declared_today_loads_\n  failed_with_structured_reason: writes a real plugin dir\n  declaring kind=\"process\" + a full [runtimes.policy] block,\n  drives scan_plugins_dir as the host CLI will at boot, asserts\n  the structured PluginFailureReason::ProcessRuntimeNotSupported\n  + the operator-facing message, asserts the registry was left\n  untouched (cross-plugin invariant), and round-trips the\n  declared policy through `.resolve()` to prove the Duration\n  shape is what the supervisor will eventually receive.\n\nAll eight tests in plugin_substrate_e2e pass; codeless-tools lib\ntests are 126/126 green; clippy on codeless-tools + codeless-\nruntime clean under -D warnings; cargo fmt --all clean.\n\n* stage 15: stage 13: codeless-server manifest parser — [[runtimes]] kind ∈ {builtin, wasm, process} with kind-specific blocks; [runtimes.capabilities]; [runtimes.policy]; two-phase scan lifted from rubix extensions-host so a partial failure cannot half-populate registries; plugin_substrate_e2e::process_runtime_declared_today_loads_failed_with_structured_reason green\n\n* stage 14: MCP-contributions surface — [contributes.mcp] parsing with strict validation; tool_call + rest_proxy dispatch kinds; parity check at load (tool_call.tool_id is a registered tool, rest_proxy.path is a registered REST route); audit event extended with plugin_id + dispatch; plugin_tools_enabled off-switch; plugin_mcp_e2e::tool_call_dispatch_round_trip + parity_rule_rejects_missing_twin + plugin_tools_off_switch_hides_listings green\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 16: stage 14: MCP-contributions surface — [contributes.mcp] parsing with strict validation; tool_call + rest_proxy dispatch kinds; parity check at load (tool_call.tool_id is a registered tool, rest_proxy.path is a registered REST route); audit event extended with plugin_id + dispatch; plugin_tools_enabled off-switch; plugin_mcp_e2e::tool_call_dispatch_round_trip + parity_rule_rejects_missing_twin + plugin_tools_off_switch_hides_listings green\n\n* stage 15: documentation + handover — PLUGIN-SUBSTRATE.md Status rows for items 9, 10, 11; resolved-OQ updates across the four plugin docs; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' lines for the three landed surfaces; final cargo test --workspace + cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings + cargo fmt --check + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green; one paragraph in the job handover summarising what''s deferred (process implementation, mcp_forward, WASM kv, hot reload, mobile MF)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 18: stage 15: documentation + handover — PLUGIN-SUBSTRATE.md Status rows for items 9, 10, 11; resolved-OQ updates across the four plugin docs; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' lines for the three landed surfaces; final cargo test --workspace + cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings + cargo fmt --check + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green; one paragraph in the job handover summarising what''s deferred (process implementation, mcp_forward, WASM kv, hot reload, mobile MF)\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"ce20c83653d716e488c330d6543f31cac3a72b4d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/git/trees/ce20c83653d716e488c330d6543f31cac3a72b4d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NubeDev/codeless/git/commits/6d8ab52604fda32d2c38d5a4e12447a679cfaaf1","verification":{"payload":"tree ce20c83653d716e488c330d6543f31cac3a72b4d\nparent cdbe584b250646b6bd68ab73a5fa6bdcea741b03\nauthor Aidan P <aidan@enviroservices.com.au> 1779144530 +0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1779144530 +0700\n\nplugin-substrate: WASM runtime, MF plugin UI (R6), process seam, MCP contributions (#33)\n\n* stage 1: resolve open questions (OQ-WASM-1, -2, -4, -5; OQ-MCP-1; OQ-UI-1; final v0.1 slot vocab) under SCOPE.md §Open questions and propagate the resolutions into the four plugin docs; no implementation\n\n* stage 0: stage 1: resolve open questions (OQ-WASM-1, -2, -4, -5; OQ-MCP-1; OQ-UI-1; final v0.1 slot vocab) under SCOPE.md §Open questions and propagate the resolutions into the four plugin docs; no implementation\n\n* stage 2: scaffold codeless-plugin-sdk (mobile-safe) with the rubix-lifted compile_error! mutual-exclusion guard for native/wasm/process features, the ToolBehavior trait, the #[derive(Tool)] macro (schemars-driven), and the register! macro stub; the existing notes plugin keeps compiling unchanged via a thin shim\n\nNew crates:\n\n- crates/codeless-plugin-sdk: mobile-safe authoring SDK. Pulls in\n  schemars, async-trait, serde, thiserror; no host deps. Carries\n  ToolMeta (compile-time id/tier/description), ToolBehavior\n  (Args/Output/call -- the trait every plugin tool implements),\n  ToolCtx (opaque seam the per-flavour adapter fills in later),\n  ToolError (variants line up 1:1 with tool-error.code in\n  tool.wit), Tier, Manifest::for_behavior::<T>() (schemars-driven\n  input/output schemas), and the register! macro stub.\n\n- crates/codeless-plugin-sdk-macros: proc-macro crate exposing\n  #[derive(Tool)] -- reads #[tool(id, tier, description)] and\n  emits the ToolMeta impl. Pattern lifted from rubix\n  extensions-sdk-macros (NodeKind); body new since codeless''s\n  manifest is schemars-derived at runtime, not embedded from YAML.\n\nThe compile_error! mutual-exclusion guard for builtin/wasm/process\nis copied from rubix-workspace/extension-sdk/extensions-sdk/src/\nlib.rs -- the failure mode is the same and a runtime-rebuilt guard\nwould diverge.\n\nregister! is intentionally a stub today. It type-checks that the\nargument implements ToolBehavior, then expands to nothing. The\nper-flavour expansion (inventory entry for builtin, wit-bindgen\nexports for wasm) lands in later stages of the\nplugin-substrate-runtimes job; plugin source written against the\nSDK today survives that with no diff.\n\nThe notes plugin keeps compiling unchanged: it still implements\ncodeless_tools::Tool directly. The SDK is parallel infrastructure\nfor the upcoming runtime-flavour work; a future stage migrates\nnotes onto ToolBehavior via the builtin-flavour host shim.\n\nPinned by crates/codeless-plugin-sdk/tests/derive_smoke.rs: derive\nemits the expected constants, Manifest::for_behavior round-trips\nschemars schemas through the manifest, ToolBehavior::call\ndispatches, and register!() compiles against a real\nToolBehavior impl.\n\nRefs: DOCS/plugins/PLUGIN-SUBSTRATE.md \"Reuse from rubix\",\nDOCS/plugins/PLUGIN-WASM.md \"The authoring SDK\".\n\n* stage 3: scaffold codeless-tool-wit with tool.wit (codeless tool ABI per PLUGIN-WASM.md), commit the wit-bindgen-generated bindings in-tree (OQ-WASM-2 resolution), and a doc-only Rust smoke test that loads the WIT and round-trips a manifest\n\n* stage 2: stage 3: scaffold codeless-tool-wit with tool.wit (codeless tool ABI per PLUGIN-WASM.md), commit the wit-bindgen-generated bindings in-tree (OQ-WASM-2 resolution), and a doc-only Rust smoke test that loads the WIT and round-trips a manifest\n\n* stage 4: scaffold codeless-plugin-host-wasm (host-only) — Wasmtime engine, WASI-p2 component-model linker, per-call instantiation, HostPolicy fuel/memory/wall-clock caps; expose a WasmAdapter implementing the runtime-adapter trait introduced in this stage in codeless-tools\n\n- Add `codeless-tools::runtime_adapter` (mobile-safe trait, table,\n  request/outcome/manifest types, collisions). Per OQ-WASM-1 the\n  table lives in `codeless-tools`; the trait carries no host-only\n  types (pure data + `async_trait`) so the iOS/Android cargo-check\n  matrix cannot leak `wasmtime` through this seam.\n- New host-only crate `codeless-plugin-host-wasm` (wasmtime 23 +\n  wasmtime-wasi 23, async + consume_fuel):\n  - `WasmRuntime` -- process-wide engine + linker prototype, WASI\n    default-deny on construction.\n  - `WasmPlugin` -- per-call instantiation; fuel/memory/deadline\n    caps from `HostPolicy` (defaults: 100M / 64 MiB / 10 s per\n    PLUGIN-WASM.md § Limits); host-side `wasmtime::component::\n    bindgen!` against `codeless-tool-wit/wit/tool.wit` for the\n    typed `describe`/`call` accessors.\n  - `WasmAdapter` -- `RuntimeAdapter` impl that forwards\n    `manifests()` and `call()` into `WasmPlugin`; one adapter per\n    plugin, N table entries pointing at the shared `Arc`.\n  - `HostPolicy::with_override` enforces \"overrides may only lower\"\n    per OQ-WASM-5 so a config-parse can fail-fast on a manifest\n    that tries to enlarge the sandbox.\n- Wasmtime trap classification (`OutOfFuel` -> `fuel`,\n  `MemoryOutOfBounds` -> `memory`, `tokio::time::timeout` ->\n  `deadline`) maps to `tool-error.code = \"limit-exceeded\"` at the\n  adapter boundary so stage 7''s end-to-end fuel test has a single\n  vocabulary to assert against.\n- Tests: runtime_adapter unit tests (register, collisions, error\n  builder); host-wasm unit tests (invalid component bytes, trap\n  classifier); host-wasm smoke test (runtime constructs, defaults\n  match doc, `RuntimeAdapter` is object-safe). End-to-end load of\n  a `.wasm` is stage 5''s owner.\n- `cargo test --workspace`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets\n  -- -D warnings`, and `cargo fmt --check` all green; codeless-\n  client''s dep tree carries no wasmtime or codeless-plugin-host-\n  wasm.\n\n* stage 3: stage 4: scaffold codeless-plugin-host-wasm (host-only) — Wasmtime engine, WASI-p2 component-model linker, per-call instantiation, HostPolicy fuel/memory/wall-clock caps; expose a WasmAdapter implementing the runtime-adapter trait introduced in this stage in codeless-tools\n\n* stage 4: M-WASM-A — codeless-plugin-sdk + codeless-tool-wit + codeless-plugin-host-wasm crates compile, the runtime-adapter table is registered in codeless-tools, no host-only types leaked into mobile-safe builds (verify via the iOS/Android cargo check matrix); no plugin loads anything yet\n\n* stage 5: port codeless-plugin-notes onto codeless-plugin-sdk -- same source, two flavours (builtin via cargo build, WASM via cargo build --target wasm32-wasip2); plugin_substrate_e2e::notes_plugin_loads_and_seeds_persona_addressable_by_thread parameterised over both flavours, both green\n\n- Rewrite NotesAppend on top of `codeless_plugin_sdk::ToolBehavior` with\n  typed `NotesAppendArgs` / `NotesAppendOutput`; the PS7 attachment marker\n  reaches `/properties/attachment/$ref` via a schemars `schema_with` hook.\n- Builtin bridge `BuiltinBridge<T: ToolBehavior>` inside the notes crate\n  adapts ToolBehavior -> `codeless_tools::Tool`, preserving the existing\n  `register` entry point. Lives in the plugin so the SDK -> host dep\n  direction stays one-way.\n- WASM flavour: cdylib + mutually-exclusive `builtin` / `wasm` features.\n  `#[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")] wasm_guest` invokes\n  `wit_bindgen::generate!`, impls `Guest::describe` / `Guest::call`\n  (pollster::block_on for the sync<->async bridge), `export!`s the\n  component. Crate-level `unsafe_code = \"deny\"` exception mirrors\n  codeless-tool-wit.\n- Bump `wasmtime` 23 -> 30 in codeless-plugin-host-wasm: LLVM 19 (rustc\n  >= 1.85) emits overlong LEB128 memory-index-0 encodings wasmparser\n  0.212 rejects; wasmtime 27+ accepts them. WasiView::table() migrated\n  to the new IoView supertrait in wasmtime-wasi 30.\n- Parameterise the substrate e2e test:\n  `notes_plugin_loads_and_seeds_persona_addressable_by_thread_builtin`\n  reads notes.append off the host ToolRegistry; the `_wasm` row builds\n  the same source via `cargo build --target wasm32-unknown-unknown\n  --no-default-features --features wasm`, composes into a WASI-p2\n  component with wit-component 0.212, and reads the manifest off a\n  WasmPlugin::load''ed component''s describe() export. Both rows pin the\n  PS7 attachment marker.\n\nNotable deviation: the e2e test builds via wasm32-unknown-unknown +\nwit-component, not wasm32-wasip2. rustc''s bundled WASI preview1->2\nadapter emits a component-model encoding wasmtime 30 still rejects\n(adapter-internal offset error); the notes plugin world has no WASI\nimports anyway, so wasm32-unknown-unknown + manual componentise is the\nclean path. PLUGIN-WASM.md doc reconciliation deferred to stage 15.\n\n* stage 5: stage 5: port codeless-plugin-notes onto codeless-plugin-sdk — same source, two flavours (builtin via cargo build, WASM via cargo build --target wasm32-wasip2); plugin_substrate_e2e::notes_plugin_loads_and_seeds_persona_addressable_by_thread parameterised over both flavours, both green\n\n* stage 6: implement WASM capability sandbox -- default-deny capability set; [runtimes.capabilities] manifest parsing; attachments R/W via the host-implemented codeless:attachments/store WIT interface; plugin_wasm_e2e::wasm_plugin_cannot_open_host_file + plugin_wasm_e2e::wasm_plugin_attachment_round_trip green\n\nPer PLUGIN-WASM.md § Capability sandbox the per-plugin linker is now\nconstructed from the manifest''s [runtimes.capabilities] grants; an\nempty set keeps every host-implemented WIT interface unlinked, so a\ncomponent importing one fails at instantiation rather than at a\ncall-time denial. New WIT packages codeless:attachments@0.1.0 +\ncodeless:fs@0.1.0 (under wit/deps/) back two companion worlds in\ntool.wit (plugin-with-attachments, plugin-with-fs) and the host\ncrate ships the AttachmentStore trait + InMemoryAttachmentStore for\nthe e2e round-trip. The plugin.toml parser gains [[runtimes]] entries\nwith strict-validated kinds (builtin / wasm / process) and a typed\ncapabilities sub-block that rejects fuel/memory/deadline at parse\ntime (OQ-WASM-5).\n\n* stage 8: implement fuel + memory + wall-clock caps; plugin_wasm_e2e::wasm_plugin_respects_fuel_cap green within the 200ms deadline; manifest accepts but the plugin cannot self-raise limits\n\nAdds the `fuel_loop` test fixture (default `plugin` world, infinite\nblack_box-guarded arithmetic loop) and the\n`wasm_plugin_respects_fuel_cap` e2e test. The test loads the\nfixture under HostPolicy { fuel = 100_000, deadline = 200 ms } and\nasserts the call surfaces ToolCallOutcome::Err with code\n`limit-exceeded` and a message naming `fuel`, completing inside\nthe 200 ms budget. The fuel/memory/deadline plumbing was already\nwired through `WasmPlugin::call` in stage 4; this stage closes the\nloop with the assertion that proves it. The manifest-side rule\n(`[runtimes.capabilities] fuel = ...` is rejected) is already\npinned by `manifest::tests::rejects_unknown_capability_field`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 7: stage 7: implement fuel + memory + wall-clock caps; plugin_wasm_e2e::wasm_plugin_respects_fuel_cap green within the 200ms deadline; manifest accepts but the plugin cannot self-raise limits\n\n* stage 8: fork rubix extension-ui-sdk into ui/codeless-ui/packages/plugin-ui-sdk/\n\nFork rubix-workspace/extension-ui-sdk@a7fecef into a new in-tree\npackage and rename the inbound imports to the codeless namespace\n(@rubix/agent-client -> @codeless/rpc, @rubix/ui-core -> @codeless/ui-core,\n@rubix/ui-kit -> @codeless/ui-kit). Every ported file carries a\n// codeless-ported-from: <rubix-path>@<sha> header so the rename is\nauditable. Rubix git history is dropped — only the source content is\nported, never .git.\n\nSet up pnpm workspaces in ui/codeless-ui/ (packages/* + the root app)\nso the new SDK package is wired into the host''s install graph. Add a\nminimal @codeless/ui-core package containing the graph-history bits\n(CommandScope, useCommandStack, useCommandStackStore, useGraphHistory,\ncreateGraphHistoryStore) ported from rubix-ui-core@59b23f4 so the\neditable-collection test bed has a working ui-core to import from.\nStage 9 fills the rest of the SDK API surface; the remaining ported\nhooks/components (useAction, useSlotWriter, BlockShell, ...) ship\nwith their codeless-ported-from headers but are excluded from\ntsconfig until @codeless/rpc and @codeless/ui-kit land.\n\nAcceptance:\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui/packages/plugin-ui-sdk test green\n  (21/21 in editable-collection.test.tsx under codeless''s vitest +\n  jsdom config — same test body as the rubix original)\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui/packages/plugin-ui-sdk typecheck clean\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui/packages/ui-core typecheck clean\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui typecheck clean (host unaffected)\n- pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green (host 118/118)\n\n* stage 9: stage 8: fork rubix extension-ui-sdk into ui/codeless-ui/packages/plugin-ui-sdk/, rename @rubix/agent-client→@codeless/rpc and @rubix/ui-core→@codeless/ui-core, drop rubix git history; every ported file carries // codeless-ported-from: <rubix-path>@<sha>; package builds and the rubix-derived editable-collection.test.tsx passes under codeless''s vitest config\n\n* stage 9: implement @codeless/plugin-ui-sdk MF surface\n\nWire the host-facing MF surface plugin authors need to ship a remote\ninto the codeless UI shell:\n\n- slots.ts: v0.1 slot vocabulary (assistant-panel, tool-result,\n  persona-picker, settings-page, composer-attachment-action) with\n  parameter parsing + ownership shape per\n  DOCS/plugins/PLUGIN-UI-FEDERATION.md.\n- mf.ts: MfRuntime install seam so the host wires\n  @module-federation/enhanced once at boot; pluginManifestUrl helper\n  keeps the /plugins/<id>/ui/mf-manifest.json layout single-sourced.\n- registration.ts: process-global slot-contribution registry with\n  subscribe/notify so a runtime enable/disable cycle re-renders\n  mounted PluginSlot instances.\n- components/PluginSlot.tsx: the single mount component the host\n  renders at every slot site; lazy-loads each contributor through\n  the installed MfRuntime, isolates failures per-contributor in an\n  error boundary, and forwards the parameterised slot arg as\n  slotArg.\n- rsbuild-shared.ts: authoritative shared-singleton pin map (React\n  19, react-dom 19, zustand 5, @tanstack/react-query 5, the three\n  @codeless/* internal packages); the source of truth for both the\n  host''s and every plugin''s rsbuild.config.ts shared field.\n- eslint-config.ts: ESLint flat-config enforcing R6 — no\n  @tauri-apps/*, no own React/zustand/tanstack-query, no fetch/XHR\n  in plugin sources; rsbuild.config.* carved out.\n\nEach module ships with tests pinning the contract host wiring and\nPLUGIN-UI-FEDERATION.md depend on (56 tests; package.json /\ntsconfig / vitest.config updated). Host UI typecheck stays green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 10: stage 9: implement @codeless/plugin-ui-sdk: mf.ts (slot vocabulary as resolved in stage 1), registration.ts, PluginSlot React component, rsbuild-shared.ts (singleton version pin source of truth), eslint-config.ts enforcing R6\n\n* stage 10: host shell wiring in ui/codeless-ui/ — rpc.plugins.list() at boot, MF host registration of each enabled plugin remote, <PluginSlot id=…/> mounted at the resolved slot sites; the host renders a fallback when a slot has no contributors\n\n* stage 11: stage 10: host shell wiring in ui/codeless-ui/ — rpc.plugins.list() at boot, MF host registration of each enabled plugin remote, <PluginSlot id=…/> mounted at the resolved slot sites; the host renders a fallback when a slot has no contributors\n\n* stage 11: server REST surface — GET /plugins reflecting the registry, GET /plugins/<id>/ui/* via ServeDir rooted at the plugin''s ui/ subdir; bearer-token auth on the listing, ServeDir matching the rest of the UI bundle''s posture\n\n* stage 12: stage 11: server REST surface — GET /plugins reflecting the registry, GET /plugins/<id>/ui/* via ServeDir rooted at the plugin''s ui/ subdir; bearer-token auth on the listing, ServeDir matching the rest of the UI bundle''s posture\n\n* stage 12: notes plugin ui/ subtree — minimal AssistantPanel remote (list recent notes via rpc.tools.call(notes.list_recent, …)) built with the plugin''s rsbuild config that imports @codeless/plugin-ui-sdk/rsbuild-shared; plugin_ui_e2e::host_loads_plugin_remote_and_mounts_assistant_panel + plugin_ui_e2e::mismatched_react_fails_loudly + plugin_ui_e2e::r6_eslint_rejects_forbidden_imports green\n\n* stage 13: stage 12: notes plugin ui/ subtree — minimal AssistantPanel remote (list recent notes via rpc.tools.call(notes.list_recent, …)) built with the plugin''s rsbuild config that imports @codeless/plugin-ui-sdk/rsbuild-shared; plugin_ui_e2e::host_loads_plugin_remote_and_mounts_assistant_panel + plugin_ui_e2e::mismatched_react_fails_loudly + plugin_ui_e2e::r6_eslint_rejects_forbidden_imports green\n\n* stage 13: codeless-server manifest parser - [[runtimes]] kind in {builtin, wasm, process} with kind-specific blocks; [runtimes.capabilities]; [runtimes.policy]; two-phase scan lifted from rubix extensions-host so a partial failure cannot half-populate registries; plugin_substrate_e2e::process_runtime_declared_today_loads_failed_with_structured_reason green\n\nManifest parser:\n- PluginRuntime gains kind-specific fields: `binary` (process) joins\n  `crate` (builtin) and `artefact` (wasm); validate_runtime_kind_block\n  enforces required-vs-not-applicable per kind so a `binary` on a\n  builtin entry fails at parse rather than being silently ignored.\n- New `[runtimes.policy]` sub-block (`PluginRuntimePolicy`):\n  socket_ready_timeout, health_interval, failure_threshold,\n  failure_window, failed_cooldown. Duration strings (\"5s\", \"100ms\",\n  \"2m\") parsed at manifest-load via a narrow `parse_duration`\n  helper. `failed_cooldown` is a TOML sum-type via `#[serde(untagged)]`\n  accepting literal `false` (sticky-Failed default) or a duration\n  string (headless retry); literal `true` rejected as meaningless.\n  Wasm and builtin entries reject any policy fields\n  (RuntimePolicyNotApplicable).\n- ResolvedPluginRuntimePolicy exposes typed Durations the future\n  process supervisor will consume; the raw manifest strings remain\n  on PluginRuntimePolicy so the serde round-trip preserves operator-\n  typed shape.\n\nTwo-phase substrate scanner (crates/codeless-tools/src/plugin/\nsubstrate.rs):\n- scan_plugins_dir(dir, &RegistrationTable) -> ScanResult walks a\n  parent dir, finds plugins via plugin.toml. Phase 1 vets each\n  manifest in isolation; phase 2 commits the vetted plugins into a\n  single PluginRegistry. A failure in either phase scopes to its\n  plugin without leaking partial state into the others, matching\n  rubix extensions-host''s two-phase shape.\n- PluginLoadOutcome::{Loaded, Failed, Unparseable} carries enough\n  state on every branch for `codeless plugin info` to render a\n  declared-but-unsupported plugin''s manifest, not just successes.\n- PluginFailureReason::ProcessRuntimeNotSupported is the\n  PLUGIN-PROCESS.md § Reserve-the-seam outcome with the stable\n  code string \"process-runtime-not-supported\"; UnknownBuiltin and\n  NoLoadableRuntime cover the other today-recoverable cases.\n- resolve_active_runtime picks builtin > wasm > process when a\n  plugin ships multiple [[runtimes]] entries.\n\nTests:\n- 12 new manifest unit tests covering required-vs-not-applicable\n  per kind, duration parsing edge cases, the failed_cooldown\n  literal-vs-duration sum-type, and policy applicability per kind.\n- 5 new substrate unit tests covering scan_plugins_dir end-to-end,\n  process-only failure, mixed-vet, unparseable manifests, and\n  unknown-builtin failure.\n- plugin_substrate_e2e::process_runtime_declared_today_loads_\n  failed_with_structured_reason: writes a real plugin dir\n  declaring kind=\"process\" + a full [runtimes.policy] block,\n  drives scan_plugins_dir as the host CLI will at boot, asserts\n  the structured PluginFailureReason::ProcessRuntimeNotSupported\n  + the operator-facing message, asserts the registry was left\n  untouched (cross-plugin invariant), and round-trips the\n  declared policy through `.resolve()` to prove the Duration\n  shape is what the supervisor will eventually receive.\n\nAll eight tests in plugin_substrate_e2e pass; codeless-tools lib\ntests are 126/126 green; clippy on codeless-tools + codeless-\nruntime clean under -D warnings; cargo fmt --all clean.\n\n* stage 15: stage 13: codeless-server manifest parser — [[runtimes]] kind ∈ {builtin, wasm, process} with kind-specific blocks; [runtimes.capabilities]; [runtimes.policy]; two-phase scan lifted from rubix extensions-host so a partial failure cannot half-populate registries; plugin_substrate_e2e::process_runtime_declared_today_loads_failed_with_structured_reason green\n\n* stage 14: MCP-contributions surface — [contributes.mcp] parsing with strict validation; tool_call + rest_proxy dispatch kinds; parity check at load (tool_call.tool_id is a registered tool, rest_proxy.path is a registered REST route); audit event extended with plugin_id + dispatch; plugin_tools_enabled off-switch; plugin_mcp_e2e::tool_call_dispatch_round_trip + parity_rule_rejects_missing_twin + plugin_tools_off_switch_hides_listings green\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 16: stage 14: MCP-contributions surface — [contributes.mcp] parsing with strict validation; tool_call + rest_proxy dispatch kinds; parity check at load (tool_call.tool_id is a registered tool, rest_proxy.path is a registered REST route); audit event extended with plugin_id + dispatch; plugin_tools_enabled off-switch; plugin_mcp_e2e::tool_call_dispatch_round_trip + parity_rule_rejects_missing_twin + plugin_tools_off_switch_hides_listings green\n\n* stage 15: documentation + handover — PLUGIN-SUBSTRATE.md Status rows for items 9, 10, 11; resolved-OQ updates across the four plugin docs; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' lines for the three landed surfaces; final cargo test --workspace + cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings + cargo fmt --check + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui test green; one paragraph in the job handover summarising what''s deferred (process implementation, mcp_forward, WASM kv, hot reload, mobile MF)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* stage 18: stage 15: documentation + handover — PLUGIN-SUBSTRATE.md Status rows for items 9, 10, 11; resolved-OQ updates across the four plugin docs; CODELESS.md ''What works today'' lines for the three landed surfaces; final cargo test --workspace + cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings + cargo fmt --check + pnpm -C ui/codeless-ui lint